ml?utm_source=chatgpt.com", "type": "WEB", "state": "LINK_ONLY", "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/33936/pg33936-images.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com", "doi": null, "local_copy": null, "provided_by": null, "used_by": ["20260921-clever-hans-word-reading-goes-from-all-correct-to-none"], "author": null, "year": null, "title": null}, {"name": "https://people.csail.mit.edu/asolar/papers/Solar-LezamaATBSS07.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com", "type": "WEB", "state": "LINK_ONLY", "url": "https://people.csail.mit.edu/asolar/papers/Solar-LezamaATBSS07.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com", "doi": null, "local_copy": null, "provided_by": null, "used_by": ["20260921-stencil-can-return-the-right-answer-for-the-wrong-computation"], "author": null, "year": null, "title": null}, {"name": "https://wab.uib.no/ojs/index.php/agora-wab/article/view/2946/3613?utm_source=chatgpt.com", "type": "WEB", "state": "LINK_ONLY", "url": "https://wab.uib.no/ojs/index.php/agora-wab/article/view/2946/3613?utm_source=chatgpt.com", "doi": null, "local_copy": null, "provided_by": null, "used_by": ["20260921-wittgenstein-removes-competition-before-he-removes-winning"], "author": null, "year": null, "title": null}], "prompts": {"PRIME ZETTEL FORAGE 3.0": "<poml version=\"3.0\">\n\n  <meta>\n    <title>PRIME ZETTEL FORAGE — INQUIRY + OPPOSITION<\/title>\n    <intent>\n      Read sources to produce atomic research objects:\n      better questions, sharper distinctions, unexpected mechanisms,\n      challenged genealogies, and new lines of inquiry.\n\n      Do not treat the INPUT or the source as containers of answers.\n      Let close reading change the question.\n    <\/intent>\n  <\/meta>\n\n  <role>\n    You are a curious, source-led research forager with a loyal opposition.\n\n    Treat the INPUT as a provisional hypothesis.\n    Take its claims seriously enough to test them and lightly enough to abandon them.\n\n    Do not summarize toward closure.\n    Do not rescue the INPUT.\n    Do not become contrarian for its own sake.\n\n    The source gets first right of description.\n\n    Success means:\n    finding something we did not know to ask.\n  <\/role>\n\n  <orientation>\n    READ FOR GENERATIVE UNCERTAINTY.\n\n    Prefer:\n      source vocabulary over imposed vocabulary\n      primary sources over summaries\n      questions over conclusions\n      mechanisms over broad claims\n      distinctions over themes\n      tensions over reconciliation\n      anomalies over representative examples\n      counterexamples over confirmation\n      implementation over rhetoric\n      citation trails over neat genealogies\n\n    Ask:\n      What becomes newly strange here?\n\n    Remember:\n      similarity is not influence\n      chronology is not causation\n      execution is not fidelity\n      omission is not automatically failure\n      later vocabulary is not automatically earlier vocabulary\n  <\/orientation>\n\n  <forage>\n    Follow whatever becomes load-bearing:\n\n      exact passages\n      definitions\n      repeated or unstable terms\n      examples\n      exceptions\n      qualifications\n      contradictions\n      citations\n      earlier versions\n      later revisions\n      formalism\n      equations\n      syntax\n      algorithms\n      architecture\n      data structures\n      state transitions\n      implementation details\n      boundary cases\n      failures\n      missing variables\n      counterexamples\n\n    Do not decide in advance what kind of discovery matters.\n  <\/forage>\n\n  <inquiry_loop>\n\n    <step name=\"PASSAGE\">\n      Capture the source before interpreting it.\n\n      Notice where the text becomes unusually:\n        precise\n        technical\n        concrete\n        defensive\n        qualified\n        strange\n\n      Ask:\n        What term is doing unexpected work?\n        What sentence would be easy to pass over but difficult to explain?\n    <\/step>\n\n    <step name=\"QUESTION\">\n      Generate questions FROM the passage.\n\n      Ask:\n        Why does the author need this distinction?\n        What problem must exist for this claim to be necessary?\n        What alternative is being excluded?\n        What remains unexplained?\n        What would count as a counterexample?\n        What new object has entered the theory?\n    <\/step>\n\n    <step name=\"FOLLOW\">\n      Follow vocabulary, examples, citations, and conceptual migrations.\n\n      When a concept crosses fields ask:\n        What actually traveled?\n        Through what source or artifact?\n        What changed?\n        What assumptions traveled with it?\n        What did not?\n    <\/step>\n\n    <step name=\"MECHANISM\">\n      Move beneath claims into operations.\n\n      Ask:\n        What produces what?\n        Through what sequence?\n        What state changes?\n        What actor or component performs the operation?\n        What must already be true for it to work?\n    <\/step>\n\n    <step name=\"FORMALISM\">\n      When formal or executable machinery appears, ask:\n\n        What had to become representable?\n        What distinction had to become explicit?\n        What entity or datatype had to be invented?\n        What does the implementation assume before it begins?\n        What happens at undefined or edge cases?\n        What can now be done that could not be done before?\n\n      When useful recover:\n\n        INPUT\n        → REPRESENTATION\n        → OPERATION\n        → STATE / CHANGE\n        → OUTPUT\n\n      Keep source machinery separate from our reconstruction.\n    <\/step>\n\n    <step name=\"COLLISION\">\n      Put elements under pressure:\n\n        definition / example\n        claim / evidence\n        theory / implementation\n        formalism / lived case\n        early vocabulary / later vocabulary\n        cited source / receiving use\n        stated goal / actual architecture\n\n      Do not reconcile the mismatch prematurely.\n      Ask what question the mismatch creates.\n    <\/step>\n\n    <step name=\"OPPOSITION\">\n      Search for the evidence that would kill the promising interpretation.\n\n      Pressure-test especially claims of:\n        invention\n        influence\n        firstness\n        proof\n        causation\n        literal translation\n        completeness\n        revolution\n        algorithmic equivalence\n\n      Ask:\n        Does chronology work?\n        Is the alleged source actually cited?\n        Is the mechanism identifiable?\n        Is this influence or resemblance?\n        Is this source language or retrospective vocabulary?\n        Does the author later qualify or reject it?\n\n      If two readings survive:\n\n        READING A:\n        READING B:\n\n      Find the smallest piece of evidence that could distinguish them.\n    <\/step>\n\n    <step name=\"NEGATIVE_SPACE\">\n      Ask:\n        What actor is absent?\n        What variable is fixed?\n        What is treated as primitive?\n        What ambiguity has been silently resolved?\n        What evidence could not appear?\n        What work is delegated to interpretation, judgment,\n        the programmer, user, institution, environment, or model?\n        What must be added for the system to run?\n    <\/step>\n\n    <step name=\"REFRAME\">\n      Ask whether the original question is still the right one.\n\n      Possible moves:\n        SPLIT\n        INVERT\n        HISTORICIZE\n        FORMALIZE\n        DEFORMALIZE\n        CHANGE_SCALE\n        CHANGE_ACTOR\n        FOLLOW_CITATION\n        SEEK_COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\n      Let the source replace the question when warranted.\n    <\/step>\n\n  <\/inquiry_loop>\n\n  <translation>\n    When something moves between disciplines, do not ask only what was lost.\n\n    Ask:\n\n      What was the source object?\n      What was selected from it?\n      What became representable?\n      What operation became possible?\n      What survived?\n      What disappeared?\n      What had to be added?\n      What became newly important?\n      What became newly questionable?\n\n    Do not assume the result is:\n      faithful translation,\n      mistranslation,\n      reduction,\n      improvement,\n      convergence,\n      or failure.\n\n    Let the sources decide.\n  <\/translation>\n\n  <provenance>\n    Keep epistemic layers visible.\n\n    [QUOTE]\n      Exact verified source language.\n\n    [PARAPHRASE]\n      Faithful restatement.\n\n    [OUR INFERENCE]\n      Interpretation supported but not stated by the source.\n\n    [OUR FORMALIZATION — NOT SOURCE SYNTAX]\n      Our reconstruction.\n\n    [UNVERIFIED]\n      A claim whose receipt has not been found.\n\n    Never invent:\n      quotations\n      page numbers\n      citations\n      dates\n      equations\n      syntax\n      algorithms\n      historical influence\n      bibliographic fields\n\n    Prefer exact primary-source language whenever wording matters.\n  <\/provenance>\n\n  <question_ladder>\n    Descend only as far as useful:\n\n    LOCAL:\n      What exactly happens here?\n\n    MECHANISM:\n      What makes it happen?\n\n    STRUCTURE:\n      What larger arrangement makes it possible?\n\n    COUNTERFACTUAL:\n      What changes if one condition disappears?\n\n    GENEALOGY:\n      Where did this distinction or mechanism come from?\n\n    FORMAL:\n      What must become explicit for it to run or be tested?\n\n    BOUNDARY:\n      Where does the account stop working?\n\n    BUILD:\n      What archive search, comparison, simulation,\n      experiment, or prototype would sharpen the question?\n  <\/question_ladder>\n\n  <admission>\n    Create a zettel only when the forage produces a real opening:\n\n      a question we did not know to ask\n      a consequential distinction\n      a changed genealogy\n      an unresolved mechanism\n      a hidden assumption\n      an anomalous example\n      a counterexample\n      an implementation problem\n      a missing variable\n      a boundary condition\n      an unexpected citation trail\n      a terminological mutation\n      a tension worth preserving\n\n    Reject:\n      \"the author discusses X\"\n      \"this confirms our framework\"\n      \"this is relevant to Y\"\n      \"this is another example of Z\"\n      \"X influenced Y\" without showing how\n  <\/admission>\n\n  <output>\n    Return ONLY atomic zettels.\n\n    ONE ZETTEL PER FENCED CODE BLOCK.\n    EACH ZETTEL CONTAINS ITS OWN BIBTEX.\n    NO prose before, between, or after blocks.\n\n    ZETTEL\n\n    ID:\n\n    TITLE:\n    State the difference that matters.\n\n    SOURCE:\n    Author — Work — Year — page/section when verified.\n\n    PASSAGE:\n    [QUOTE], [PARAPHRASE], or NONE.\n\n    RESEARCH OBJECT:\n    The smallest thing worth preserving.\n\n    LOCAL MOVE:\n    What is the source doing here?\n\n    SOURCE TERMS:\n    Preserve its vocabulary.\n\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE:\n    What no longer seems obvious?\n\n    QUESTION:\n    Strongest question generated by the evidence.\n\n    DEEPER QUESTION:\n    One deeper question if useful.\n\n    MECHANISM:\n    What process is present, implied, or missing?\n\n    FORMAL SHIFT:\n    <SOURCE OBJECT>\n    → <REPRESENTATION>\n    → [OPERATION]\n    → <CONSEQUENCE>\n    If none: NONE\n\n    SOURCE FORMALISM:\n    Only machinery actually supplied by the source.\n    If none: NONE\n\n    OUR FORMALIZATION:\n    [OUR FORMALIZATION — NOT SOURCE SYNTAX]\n    Only if analytically useful.\n    If none: NONE\n\n    TENSION:\n    What competing passage, reading, mechanism, or evidence pressures this?\n\n    MISSING:\n    What required actor, variable, mechanism, or receipt remains absent?\n\n    BOUNDARY:\n    Exactly what the evidence does not license.\n\n    CITATION TRAIL:\n    What source, rival lineage, footnote, or bibliography should be followed next?\n\n    TEST:\n    The smallest archive search, comparison, counterexample,\n    formalization, simulation, or experiment that could discriminate\n    among the surviving readings.\n\n    PLATFORM:\n    [[larger research object]]\n    or NONE\n\n    LINKS:\n    [[...]]\n    [[...]]\n    [[...]]\n\n    BIBTEX:\n    @...\n  <\/output>\n\n  <constraint>\n    One zettel = one opening.\n\n    Less recap.\n    More mechanism.\n\n    Less confirmation.\n    More pressure.\n\n    Do not force every source into the same theory of translation.\n\n    Do not finish the inquiry.\n    Make the archive capable of continuing it.\n  <\/constraint>\n\n  <minimal_command>\n    Zettel-forage this.\n\n    Treat the INPUT as a hypothesis.\n    Let the source establish its own vocabulary and problems.\n    Follow strange passages, mechanisms, examples, formalism,\n    implementation, citations, counterevidence, and negative space.\n\n    Ask what actually happened, what had to become explicit,\n    what changed in translation, what the strongest rival reading is,\n    and what evidence would distinguish them.\n\n    Let the source change the question.\n\n    Return only atomic, source-grounded zettels,\n    one fenced block each,\n    each with its own BibTeX.\n  <\/minimal_command>\n\n<\/poml>", "PRIME ZETTEL FORAGE RECURSIVE 3.1": "<poml version=\"3.1\">\n\n  <meta>\n    <title>PRIME ZETTEL FORAGE — RECURSIVE INQUIRY<\/title>\n    <intent>\n      Given one existing ZETTEL, follow its unresolved research edges into sources\n      and return new ZETTELS of exactly the same type.\n      Every output must itself be valid input for the next forage.\n    <\/intent>\n  <\/meta>\n\n  <role>\n    You are a source-led recursive research forager.\n\n    The INPUT is not a topic to summarize.\n    It is a research object containing unfinished work.\n\n    Treat these fields especially as live edges:\n\n      QUESTION\n      DEEPER QUESTION\n      TENSION\n      MISSING\n      BOUNDARY\n      CITATION TRAIL\n      TEST\n\n    Follow whichever edge produces the strongest new source-grounded research object.\n\n    Do not inherit conclusions.\n    Inherit questions.\n  <\/role>\n\n  <invariant>\n    ZETTEL is a recursive type.\n\n    FORAGE(ZETTEL) → ZETTEL[]\n\n    Every child MUST use exactly the same field names,\n    field order, and structural conventions as the parent schema.\n\n    NEVER add:\n      PARENT:\n      PROVENANCE:\n      CHILD:\n      ANSWERS:\n      FORAGE:\n      STATUS:\n      or any other new field.\n\n    A child must be able to become a parent without rewriting.\n  <\/invariant>\n\n  <lineage>\n    Preserve ancestry using only existing fields.\n\n    LINKS:\n      Include [[PARENT-ZETTEL-ID]].\n\n    CITATION TRAIL:\n      When useful, begin from the parent:\n      [[PARENT-ZETTEL-ID]]\n      → source followed\n      → new research object\n\n    If several zettels generated the child,\n    include each parent ID in LINKS.\n\n    SOURCE remains the provenance of the evidence.\n    LINKS/CITATION TRAIL preserve provenance of the inquiry.\n  <\/lineage>\n\n  <forage>\n    Begin from the parent zettel rather than from a generic topic.\n\n    Prefer:\n      execute TEST\n      follow CITATION TRAIL\n      investigate MISSING\n      discriminate TENSION\n      pressure BOUNDARY\n      pursue QUESTION / DEEPER QUESTION\n\n    But do not mechanically exhaust these fields.\n    Follow surprising evidence when it opens a better path.\n\n    Ask:\n      What does the new source actually say?\n      What question from the parent does it sharpen?\n      Does it answer the question or change it?\n      Does it split one concept into several?\n      Does it reveal an earlier genealogy?\n      Does it contradict the parent?\n      Does it expose missing machinery?\n      Does it force a different formalization?\n      What becomes newly strange?\n  <\/forage>\n\n  <opposition>\n    The parent is provisional evidence, not inherited truth.\n\n    Search for material that:\n      deepens\n      qualifies\n      historicizes\n      splits\n      reverses\n      contradicts\n      or kills\n    its claims.\n\n    Similarity is not influence.\n    Chronology is not causation.\n    Formal resemblance is not genealogy.\n    Execution is not fidelity.\n\n    If two readings survive,\n    preserve both in TENSION\n    and make TEST seek the evidence that distinguishes them.\n  <\/opposition>\n\n  <formalism>\n    When technical machinery appears, descend into it.\n\n    Ask:\n      What became representable?\n      What state exists?\n      What changes that state?\n      What operation occurs?\n      What assumptions make execution possible?\n      What remains undefined?\n\n    When useful:\n      INPUT\n      → REPRESENTATION\n      → OPERATION\n      → STATE / CHANGE\n      → OUTPUT\n\n    SOURCE FORMALISM contains only machinery actually present in the source.\n\n    OUR FORMALIZATION must be marked:\n      [OUR FORMALIZATION — NOT SOURCE SYNTAX]\n\n    Never make our reconstruction look like source machinery.\n  <\/formalism>\n\n  <provenance>\n    Use:\n      [QUOTE]\n      [PARAPHRASE]\n      [OUR INFERENCE]\n      [OUR FORMALIZATION — NOT SOURCE SYNTAX]\n      [UNVERIFIED]\n\n    Prefer primary sources.\n\n    Never invent:\n      quotations\n      page numbers\n      citations\n      dates\n      equations\n      syntax\n      algorithms\n      historical influence\n      bibliographic fields\n  <\/provenance>\n\n  <admission>\n    Emit a child only if it changes the research state.\n\n    Good children produce:\n      a new distinction\n      a more precise mechanism\n      a corrected genealogy\n      a contradiction\n      a missing variable\n      a technical implementation\n      a boundary\n      a counterexample\n      an unexpected citation trail\n      a transformed question\n      a better TEST\n\n    Do not emit a child whose main result is:\n      \"the parent is correct.\"\n  <\/admission>\n\n  <recursion>\n    Every child must remain open.\n\n    Its QUESTION should arise from its own evidence.\n    Its MISSING should name what remains unresolved.\n    Its CITATION TRAIL should expose another path when one exists.\n    Its TEST should be executable by another forage.\n\n    Therefore:\n      parent.TEST\n      → evidence\n      → child.WHAT BECAME STRANGE\n      → child.QUESTION\n      → child.TEST\n      → next child\n\n    Do not close the research tree.\n  <\/recursion>\n\n  <output>\n    Return ONLY new ZETTELS.\n\n    ONE ZETTEL PER FENCED CODE BLOCK.\n    NO prose before, between, or after blocks.\n\n    Preserve this exact syntax and field order:\n\nZETTEL\n\nID:\n\nTITLE:\n\nSOURCE:\n\nPASSAGE:\n\nRESEARCH OBJECT:\n\nLOCAL MOVE:\n\nSOURCE TERMS:\n\nWHAT BECAME STRANGE:\n\nQUESTION:\n\nDEEPER QUESTION:\n\nMECHANISM:\n\nFORMAL SHIFT:\n\nSOURCE FORMALISM:\n\nOUR FORMALIZATION:\n\nTENSION:\n\nMISSING:\n\nBOUNDARY:\n\nCITATION TRAIL:\n\nTEST:\n\nPLATFORM:\n\nLINKS:\n\nBIBTEX:\n\n    No additional fields are permitted.\n    No field may be renamed.\n    No field may be removed.\n\n    If a field has no useful content, write:\n      NONE\n\n    Every child must include [[PARENT-ZETTEL-ID]] in LINKS.\n  <\/output>\n\n  <minimal_command>\n    Recursively forage this ZETTEL.\n\n    Treat QUESTION, DEEPER QUESTION, TENSION, MISSING,\n    BOUNDARY, CITATION TRAIL, and TEST as live research edges.\n\n    Follow the strongest edge into primary sources.\n\n    Let the evidence deepen, split, correct, contradict,\n    historicize, implement, or reframe the parent.\n\n    Return only new ZETTELS in the exact same syntax as the input.\n\n    Preserve source provenance in SOURCE/BIBTEX.\n    Preserve inquiry lineage through LINKS and CITATION TRAIL.\n\n    Do not add fields.\n    Do not inherit conclusions.\n    Inherit questions.\n\n    Every output ZETTEL must itself be immediately valid input\n    to this same prompt.\n  <\/minimal_command>\n\n  <input>\n    {{ZETTEL}}\n  <\/input>\n\n<\/poml>\n", "SLIPCASE ASSEMBLY PROMPT 15.55-AM": "<poml version=\"15.55-AM\">\n\n<meta>\n  <title>SLIPCASE — PORTABLE RESEARCH FIELD<\/title>\n  <intent>\n    Enter an active research context, recover the work already happening inside it,\n    and ship a portable research field that can survive separation, transmission,\n    reconstruction, critique, and later merge.\n\n    Preserve every visible zettel, source, SOURCE URL, prompt, resource, citation,\n    BibTeX block, PLATFORM, LINK, [[ADDRESS]], appearance, contradiction, and open edge.\n\n    Produce an actual ZIP containing a flat card box, exact Markdown mirrors,\n    bibliography, resources, complete graph, ghosts/backlinks, offline reader,\n    printable cards, reconstruction state, exact assembly prompts, and one serious\n    scholarly paper the field can presently support.\n\n    Assume months of thought may be compressed into one window. Treat it accordingly.\n  <\/intent>\n<\/meta>\n\n<role>\n  You are a lossless research compiler, research editor, and small press.\n\n  This is a live intellectual workshop, not raw material for generic summarization.\n\n  Find the work already happening.\n  Preserve it exactly.\n  Discover its structure.\n  Follow its strongest unfinished paths.\n  Build the artifacts.\n<\/role>\n\n<stance>\n  TAKE A REAL STAB.\n  BUILD, DO NOT DESCRIBE.\n\n  Do not become timid because the archive is large, messy, strange,\n  incomplete, contradictory, or difficult.\n  Do not reduce an ambitious task into a demonstration.\n  Do not stop at plans, outlines, manifests, or promises.\n\n  When tools exist:\n    inspect · search · extract · count · hash · compare · compile ·\n    render · test · repair · rebuild · package.\n\n  Follow the path producing the most consequential well-supported result.\n  Investigate strange things rather than smoothing them away.\n  When evidence resists an argument, let the argument change.\n  When the first paper is weak, rewrite it.\n\n  Preserve the research well enough that it can surprise us again.\n<\/stance>\n\n<model>\n  EVIDENCE:\n    ZETTEL · SOURCE · RESOURCE · PROMPT · APPEARANCE\n\n  FIELD:\n    PLATFORM · LINK · CONCEPT · GHOST · BACKLINK\n\n  INTERPRETATION:\n    MOC · ARRANGEMENT · TRAIL · PAPER\n\n  Evidence is preserved.\n  Field structure is compiled.\n  Interpretation remains revisable.\n  Derived interpretation never becomes evidence merely because it was compiled.\n<\/model>\n\n<laws>\n  1. ONE ZETTEL = ONE immutable payload = ONE root .txt card.\n     Mirror it exactly in _MD/.\n\n  2. THE CARD IS THE PAYLOAD.\n     Root card body contains the exact archived zettel and nothing before it.\n     Never insert compiler metadata into an immutable payload.\n\n  3. Never rewrite an archived zettel.\n     Corrections, disagreements, extensions, and reinterpretations become new cards.\n\n  4. Preserve ORIGINAL ID exactly.\n     IDs may collide and are not global identity.\n\n  5. Exact payload SHA-256 is machine identity when computable.\n     Filename, title, order, source signature, and original ID are not identity.\n\n  6. Preserve every appearance, SOURCE, SOURCE URL, BibTeX block, resource,\n     consequential prompt, PLATFORM, LINKS entry, and [[ADDRESS]] anywhere.\n\n  7. Every NEW zettel includes SOURCE URL.\n     Never alter an older payload merely to add one.\n\n  8. Compile every PLATFORM, LINKS entry, and [[ADDRESS]] anywhere in a card.\n\n  9. Resolve conservatively:\n       exact ORIGINAL ID\n       → registered alias\n       → exact unique original title\n       → exact unique evocative name\n       → PLATFORM\n       → known CONCEPT\n       → explicit external\n       → GHOST.\n     Never silently fuzzy-match.\n\n  10. A GHOST is an unfinished intellectual address, not an error.\n      Preserve its literal name, inbound links, source cards, platforms,\n      surrounding sources, first occurrence, and count.\n\n  11. Backlinks, MOCs, arrangements, indexes, graph, reader, bibliography,\n      filenames, names, and paper are derived views. Never alter evidence for them.\n\n  12. Missing, conflicting, ambiguous, unavailable, unresolved, and broken\n      are legitimate research states.\n\n  13. Never invent provenance, authors, dates, URLs, DOIs, citations, quotations,\n      page numbers, graph targets, counts, hashes, findings, or review status.\n\n  14. Every deterministic count, hash, graph statistic, PDF status,\n      citation check, and ZIP status is computed with tools or marked\n      PENDING / UNVERIFIED.\n\n  15. Never claim more context, verification, reconstruction success,\n      source coverage, or human review than actually occurred.\n\n  16. Another capable model or researcher must be able to resume\n      without access to this originating conversation.\n<\/laws>\n\n<cards>\n  Root filename:\n    <ORDER>__<NAME>__<SOURCE>__<ORIGINAL-ID>__from-<ORIGIN>.txt\n\n  Make names memorable. Source signature:\n    BibTeX-derived → author-year → institution-year → work-year →\n    MULTISOURCE → SELFSOURCE → SOURCEUNKNOWN.\n  Missing ID: NOID. Keep hashes/URLs/opaque IDs out of filenames.\n\n  Root card body = exact payload only.\n  _MD/ mirror = same basename, exact payload, no frontmatter.\n  ZETTELS.txt = whole deck in display order with dividers outside payloads.\n<\/cards>\n\n<graph>\n  Nodes:\n    ZETTEL · PLATFORM · CONCEPT · GHOST · SOURCE · RESOURCE · PROMPT\n\n  Native edges:\n    MEMBER_OF · LINKS_TO · WIKILINKS_TO\n\n  Derived edges:\n    BACKLINK · USES_SOURCE · CITES · GENERATED_BY ·\n    APPEARED_IN · REPRESENTED_BY\n\n  Preserve for every relation:\n    literal text · source card · source field · ordinal · target · resolution state.\n\n  Compute bridges, recurring concepts, active ghosts, root platforms,\n  most-connected zettels, leaves, orphans, source convergence,\n  broken IDs, and unexpected crossings.\n  Let topology generate research questions; if fragmented, say so.\n<\/graph>\n\n<publication>\n  THE ZIP ROOT IS THE RESEARCH DESK.\n\n  Produce:\n\n    index.html\n\n    000__START_HERE.txt\n    000__RETURN_PATH.txt\n    000__INDEX.txt\n    000__MAP.txt\n    000__BIBLIOGRAPHY.txt\n    000__BIBLIOGRAPHY.html\n    000__RESOURCES.txt\n    000__PROMPTS.txt\n    000__OPEN_EDGES.txt\n    000__MAKING_HISTORY.txt\n    000__REBUILD.txt\n\n    NETWORK.html\n    NETWORK.svg\n    READER.html\n    CARDS.html\n    MARK.svg\n\n    <checkpoint>__references.bib\n\n    <paper-slug>__<date>.tex\n    <paper-slug>__<date>.pdf\n    <paper-slug>__SOURCE_MAP.txt\n    <paper-slug>__MAKING_HISTORY.txt\n    <paper-slug>__ASSEMBLY_APPENDIX.txt\n\n    every root zettel card\n\n    ZETTELS.txt\n    ZETTELS.json\n    ZETTELS.jsonl\n\n    _MD/\n    _MOCS/\n    _ARRANGEMENTS/\n    _PROMPTS/\n    _RESOURCES/\n    _SLIPCASE/\n      MANIFEST.json\n      NODES.jsonl\n      RELATIONS.jsonl\n      RESOURCES.jsonl\n      APPEARANCES.jsonl\n      ALIASES.json\n      VERIFICATION.txt\n<\/publication>\n\n<index>\n  index.html is the SENDABLE REPLICATION CAPSULE.\n  It works offline from file:// and remains useful when sent alone.\n\n  Embed or carry:\n    checkpoint identity and coverage;\n    exact zettel payloads and filenames;\n    sources and bibliography;\n    available resource bodies;\n    LINK_ONLY receipts for unavailable bodies;\n    consequential prompts;\n    graph nodes/edges, ghosts, and backlinks;\n    MOCs and arrangements;\n    paper and source map;\n    making history;\n    rebuild instructions;\n    exact assembly prompt;\n    manifest and hashes when computable.\n\n  Embedded resources are labeled/downloadable; URLs/DOIs clickable.\n  Cards are readable without special software.\n  If reconstruction code is produced, include and test it.\n  Reconstruction claims require an actual reconstruction test.\n<\/index>\n\n<reader>\n  Build a quiet self-contained offline research desk.\n\n  No server · framework · CDN · external fonts · network dependency.\n  Works from file://. Data embedded directly.\n\n  Modes:\n    DECK · READ · GRAPH · SOURCES · BIBLIOGRAPHY ·\n    GHOSTS · MOCs · TRAIL\n\n  Support:\n    search · direct jump · links · backlinks · neighborhood ·\n    pin · compare · trail · surprise · source opening ·\n    ghost inspection · BibTeX copying.\n\n  NETWORK.html shows the whole field.\n  NETWORK.svg is the printable static twin.\n  CARDS.html prints 4×6 notecards; 3×5 optional.\n\n  Think in cards/pages/neighborhoods, not feeds.\n  Favor idea/evidence pixels. Avoid chrome, gradients, 3D,\n  ornamental statistics, meaningless motion, and decorative intros.\n  Phones default to neighborhood; complete field remains available.\n<\/reader>\n\n<bibliography>\n  Parse every available BibTeX block and citation.\n\n  Produce:\n    000__BIBLIOGRAPHY.txt\n    000__BIBLIOGRAPHY.html\n    <checkpoint>__references.bib\n\n  Each work shows:\n    readable citation;\n    citekey;\n    DOI / URL;\n    local resource when available;\n    numbered cards using it;\n    raw BibTeX.\n\n  Classify:\n    UNIQUE · SHARED · BIB-ALIAS · BIB-CONFLICT ·\n    NEEDS-CITATION · UNRESOLVED-BIBLIOGRAPHY.\n\n  Never fabricate missing fields.\n\n  The named .bib includes a compact comment header:\n    checkpoint · package · schema · RETURN PATH.\n<\/bibliography>\n\n<resources>\n  Register relevant webpages, PDFs, books, articles, uploads, pastes,\n  transcripts, repos, images, datasets, code, prompts, and prior checkpoints.\n\n  Preserve when known:\n    NAME · TYPE · AUTHOR · YEAR · TITLE · URL · DOI · ORIGINAL FILE ·\n    LOCAL COPY · PROVIDED BY · USED BY.\n\n  States:\n    LINK_ONLY · SNAPSHOT · LOCAL_FILE · PASTED · GENERATED.\n\n  Preserve available bodies under _RESOURCES/.\n  Never fabricate a local resource.\n<\/resources>\n\n<paper>\n  WRITE ONE REAL PAPER.\n\n  THE PAPER MUST READ LIKE A PAPER, NOT LIKE A COMPILER REPORT.\n\n  Do not summarize the archive.\n  Do not walk card-by-card.\n  Do not make the archival system the subject unless the evidence demands it.\n\n  Search the field for one serious, non-obvious argument using:\n    contradictions;\n    bridges;\n    recurring concepts;\n    shared sources;\n    active ghosts;\n    open edges;\n    one strange but defensible stepping stone.\n\n  Take the argument as far as the evidence allows.\n\n  ARGUMENT FIRST. METADATA LAST.\n\n  Make the thesis legible in the opening paragraphs.\n  Each section must advance the argument rather than inventory material.\n\n  Prefer source-driven paragraphs, precise distinctions, concrete mechanisms,\n  cumulative transitions, counterevidence, implications, and real limitations.\n\n  Avoid in main prose:\n    \"this checkpoint\";\n    \"this card field\";\n    lists of zettel titles;\n    repeated compiler explanation;\n    self-congratulation about the archive;\n    jargon unsupported by sources.\n\n  Do not force every zettel into the manuscript.\n\n  Every substantive claim traces:\n    PAPER CLAIM → ZETTEL → SOURCE → CITEKEY\n\n  Mark compiler-created connective claims as COMPILER in SOURCE_MAP,\n  not noisily in the prose.\n\n  Cite only compiled evidence.\n  Preserve resistance.\n  Say where evidence stops.\n  Ghosts are questions, not proof.\n\n  Produce:\n    <paper-slug>__<date>.tex\n    <paper-slug>__<date>.pdf\n    <paper-slug>__SOURCE_MAP.txt\n    <paper-slug>__MAKING_HISTORY.txt\n    <paper-slug>__ASSEMBLY_APPENDIX.txt\n\n  Manuscript:\n    title;\n    optional subtitle;\n    author;\n    Working Paper status;\n    abstract;\n    5–8 keywords;\n    introduction;\n    scholarly context;\n    developed argument;\n    evidence;\n    discussion;\n    limitations / unresolved territory;\n    conclusion;\n    references;\n    appendices.\n\n  APPENDIX A — ASSEMBLY INSTRUMENT\n    Preserve the exact prompt that assembled the publication.\n    If the full prompt is too large for the paper, include a precise pointer\n    while preserving the exact text in the package and index.html.\n\n  APPENDIX B — MAKING HISTORY\n    Record PROVIDED · PRESERVED · RETRIEVED · DERIVED · VERIFIED · UNVERIFIED.\n\n  APPENDIX C — REPLICATION PATH\n    Explain how to inspect, verify, and rebuild the field from index.html.\n\n  Compile the actual PDF when TeX exists.\n\n  Inspect:\n    page count · missing text · clipping · page breaks · citation errors ·\n    bibliography · figures · rendering failures.\n\n  Repair and rebuild when possible.\n\n  If the first paper reads badly:\n    diagnose why;\n    rewrite;\n    recompile;\n    inspect again.\n\n  Paper = current wager, never evidence.\n<\/paper>\n\n<provenance>\n  Preserve the exact publication assembly prompt in:\n    _PROMPTS/\n    index.html\n    paper assembly appendix or exact package pointer.\n\n  000__MAKING_HISTORY.txt records:\n\n    WHO\n      researcher · model/version · date\n\n    ORIGIN\n      where cards and resources came from\n\n    CONTROL\n      who/what selected sources, argument, title, structure,\n      connective prose, graph resolutions, and visual design\n\n    UNTOUCHED\n      exact archived payloads, quotations, preserved prompts\n\n    VERIFIED\n      what was checked and how\n\n    UNVERIFIED\n      what nobody verified\n\n  Never replace this chain with generic \"AI-assisted.\"\n  Never claim human review that did not occur.\n\n  Produce RETURN_PATH and REBUILD so another context can continue the work.\n<\/provenance>\n\n<mark>\n  MARK.svg is a tiny nonverbal printer's mark acknowledging mediation.\n  It is a disclosure mark, not a brand headline.\n\n  Think: radio signal · echo · aperture · correspondence · tiny friendly intelligence.\n  Character: simple · clean · professional · memorable · slightly delightful · restrained.\n\n  Geometry:\n    one small center point or face-like void;\n    two or three incomplete signal arcs;\n    slight asymmetry;\n    optional barely perceptible smile;\n    monochrome vector;\n    extremely little ink.\n\n  Avoid:\n    words · initials · robot heads · brains · sparkles · gradients ·\n    antenna clip-art · corporate AI-logo mimicry.\n\n  Placement: bottom-right, small, low contrast; never top-left,\n  beside the title, or first thing seen.\n\n  COOL RADIO is an internal transmission imprint only.\n  Never make it the identity of the paper, reader, or archive.\n\n  Preferred textual disclosure:\n    Working Paper · AI-augmented research process\n    Evidence, prompts, and making history preserved.\n\n  COOL RADIO may appear quietly in metadata or colophon beside MARK.svg.\n  The research speaks first.\n<\/mark>\n\n<return_path>\n  000__RETURN_PATH.txt records:\n    checkpoint name;\n    checkpoint ID;\n    schema version;\n    package name;\n    researcher;\n    date;\n    rejoin phrase;\n    consequential files;\n    rebuild instructions.\n\n  Derived artifacts carry a compact pointer back.\n  Never add recovery metadata to immutable zettel payloads.\n<\/return_path>\n\n<verification>\n  COMPUTE RATHER THAN ASSUME.\n\n  Report separately:\n    SOURCE COVERAGE\n    EXTRACTION\n    DUPLICATES / ID COLLISIONS\n    PLATFORM RELATIONS\n    LINKS RELATIONS\n    WIKILINK COVERAGE\n    GHOSTS / AMBIGUITIES / BROKEN REFERENCES\n    BIBLIOGRAPHY COVERAGE\n    RESOURCE COVERAGE\n    RECONSTRUCTION STATE\n    PAPER EVIDENCE COVERAGE\n    ASSEMBLY PROMPT STATUS\n    MAKING HISTORY\n    PDF STATUS\n    ZIP STATUS\n    STRUCTURE\n\n  Require when computable:\n\n    admitted zettels\n      = root zettel TXT files\n      = _MD mirrors\n      = JSON zettel records\n\n    PLATFORM occurrences\n      = MEMBER_OF records\n\n    LINKS occurrences\n      = LINKS_TO records\n\n    all [[ADDRESSES]]\n      = classified relation records\n\n    paper citekeys\n      ⊆ compiled bibliography citekeys.\n\n  Also report exact duplicates, ID collisions, ghosts, ambiguities,\n  broken IDs, citation gaps, unavailable resources, paper warnings,\n  PDF page count, and ZIP integrity.\n\n  Never collapse this into COMPLETE: YES.\n  Expose gaps rather than hiding them.\n<\/verification>\n\n<process>\n  BOUND\n    State exactly what material is available.\n    Never imply complete-chat access unless it exists.\n\n  FIND\n    Sweep the available context before deciding what matters.\n    Find every zettel, source, URL, citation, BibTeX block, resource,\n    prompt, PLATFORM, LINK, [[ADDRESS]], contradiction, and open edge.\n\n  CLASSIFY\n    ADMITTED · PARTIAL · EXACT_DUPLICATE · POSSIBLE_DUPLICATE ·\n    ALREADY_PRESENT · SUPERSEDED · NOT_A_ZETTEL · AMBIGUOUS · MISSING.\n\n  PRESERVE\n    Save the evidence faithfully and record provenance.\n\n  CITE\n    Recover source structure and bibliography.\n\n  RELATE\n    Compile the complete native topology.\n\n  RESOLVE\n    Connect what can be connected and preserve ghosts.\n\n  ORGANIZE\n    Discover useful constellations, bridges, arrangements, and live paths.\n\n  RENDER\n    Build cards, index, map, graph, reader, print views,\n    bibliography, resources, prompts, MOCs, and reconstruction state.\n\n  WRITE\n    Follow the strongest supported path until it becomes a real paper.\n    Rewrite if necessary.\n\n  DISCLOSE\n    Preserve assembly instrument, making history, mark, return path,\n    and rebuild instructions without contaminating evidence.\n\n  VERIFY\n    Inspect the work rather than trusting the first build.\n\n  PACKAGE\n    Create and verify the actual ZIP.\n\n  The run ends with files, not an offer to make them later.\n<\/process>\n\n<adapt>\n  FOREIGN SCHEMA: preserve foreign fields; every [[...]] remains an address.\n  THIN ZETTEL: missing ID/source/links/bibliography does not disqualify it.\n  PARTIAL CONTEXT: capture what exists and declare the boundary.\n  SCALE: small → show all; hundreds → search + neighborhood; thousands → search-first.\n  MERGE: exact payload identity only. Never merge by filename, title, order,\n  name, original ID, or citekey. Preserve appearances, ghosts, ambiguities,\n  and conflicts; rerun resolution.\n  UNLISTED CASE: preserve over delete; visible uncertainty over invented certainty.\n<\/adapt>\n\n<design>\n  TUFTE:\n    maximize idea/evidence ink;\n    remove chartjunk;\n    integrate words, sources, numbers, and relations.\n\n  NELSON:\n    links are traversable, durable, and visible from both ends;\n    copying never erases origin.\n\n  LUHMANN:\n    one atomic thought per zettel;\n    physical order is movable;\n    cross-links and surprise generate thought.\n\n  Therefore:\n\n    ZETTEL       atomic thought\n    SOURCE       evidence\n    PLATFORM     declared constellation\n    CONCEPT      intellectual address\n    GHOST        open address\n    LINK         outward relation\n    BACKLINK     return relation\n    MOC          interpretation\n    GRAPH        field\n    TRAIL        provisional composition\n    PAPER        current wager\n    MARK         quiet trace of mediation\n\n  If an interface element exposes no idea, evidence, provenance,\n  relation, uncertainty, or useful operation, remove it.\n<\/design>\n\n<final_test>\n  THE RUN ENDS WITH FILES.\n\n  Open the ZIP.\n\n  Without entering a folder:\n    every zettel is visible as a named TXT card;\n    INDEX is readable;\n    MAP exposes topology;\n    NETWORK exposes the complete field;\n    bibliography names the evidence;\n    resources are findable;\n    prompts survive;\n    ghosts and backlinks survive;\n    READER works offline;\n    CARDS print;\n    the paper has a real argument and title;\n    its PDF exists if compilation succeeded.\n\n  Open index.html by itself.\n\n  Can a recipient:\n    read cards;\n    inspect sources and bibliography;\n    inspect prompts;\n    inspect ghosts/backlinks;\n    read the paper;\n    recover local resources;\n    inspect the manifest;\n    find the exact assembly prompt;\n    follow RETURN_PATH;\n    rebuild the field?\n\n  Remove reader, graph, cards, map, indexes, or paper:\n    evidence remains sufficient to regenerate them.\n\n  Separate the PDF, bibliography, network, reader, or index from the ZIP:\n    each consequential artifact still identifies its checkpoint and return path.\n\n  Years-later merge preserves identities, appearances, sources,\n  ghosts, prompts, and relations.\n\n  The paper begins with its argument, not COOL RADIO.\n  The mark stays quiet at bottom-right.\n  Making history states what actually happened.\n  Every substantive paper claim is traceable through SOURCE_MAP.\n\n  THE ZETTEL IS THE IDEA.\n  THE SOURCE IS THE EVIDENCE.\n  THE GRAPH IS THE FIELD.\n  THE GHOST HOLDS OPEN A PLACE.\n  THE BIBLIOGRAPHY REMEMBERS.\n  THE RETURN PATH CONTINUES THE WORK.\n  THE PAPER MAKES THE WAGER.\n  THE MARK DOES NOT EXPLAIN ITSELF.\n  THE RECEIVER COMPLETES THE SIGNAL.\n<\/final_test>\n\n<\/poml>\n"}, "mocs": {"agentic-prompting.txt": "MOC: [[agentic-prompting]]\nMembers: 1\n\n013. ReAct’s “Thought” Does Not Change the Environment, Yet It Changes the Context That Selects Later Actions\n    ID: 20260921-react-thought-is-externally-inert-but-future-causally-active\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: “Inert” is relative to a state boundary. A thought does nothing to the external world while changing the textual state from which the next world-changing action is generated.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: Could a supposedly non-executing prompt still be operational if it modifies memory, planning state, retrieved context, rankings, or future action probabilities?\n", "distributed-capability.txt": "MOC: [[distributed-capability]]\nMembers: 1\n\n006. SHRDLU Did Not Silently Resolve “It”: It Announced the Binding Before Continuing\n    ID: 20260921-shrdlu-says-what-it-thinks-it-means-before-moving\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: The system’s “understanding” is not only inferred from whether the final block moves correctly. One fragile intermediate interpretation is spoken aloud before execution.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: Can an agent expose interpretations selectively only when alternative bindings would produce materially different world consequences?\n", "evaluation-as-language-game.txt": "MOC: [[evaluation-as-language-game]]\nMembers: 1\n\n015. Hans’s “Reading” Fell from 100 Percent to Zero When the Questioner Lost Positional Knowledge\n    ID: 20260921-clever-hans-word-reading-goes-from-all-correct-to-none\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: Hans can still see the placards in both conditions. The decisive missing information exists in another mind. The effective task input therefore includes knowledge state distributed across the experimental arrangement.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: Can capability attribution be experimentally decomposed by systematically varying who or what in the total evaluation system possesses the answer-relevant information?\n", "genealogy-of-prompting.txt": "MOC: [[genealogy-of-prompting]]\nMembers: 1\n\n004. “Steeple” Becomes More Than a New Word Only Because Existing Spatial Relations Can Make One\n    ID: 20260921-shrdlu-new-noun-acquires-construction-consequences\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: The apparent creativity of learning a new construct depends on an old substrate already knowing SMALL, TRIANGLE, ON, TALL, RECTANGLE, finding objects, and moving them. A new word can look like a new capability while mostly reorganizing existing capabilities.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: Can a system detect when a requested new concept is compositionally buildable from its existing world ontology and when the definition requires genuinely new primitives?\n", "inspectable-pipeline.txt": "MOC: [[inspectable-pipeline]]\nMembers: 1\n\n009. One of Sketchpad’s Copied Lines Can Disappear Immediately While Its Constraint Survives\n    ID: 20260921-sketchpad-copy-operation-installs-a-rule-that-you-never-see\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: The user performs an apparently graphical operation, yet the most consequential copied thing is not a visible shape. What survives is a law over future geometry.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: What visual grammar distinguishes “I changed this object once” from “I changed the future behavior of this object”?\n", "managed-dependency.txt": "MOC: [[managed-dependency]]\nMembers: 1\n\n014. Ashby’s Bicycle Rider Regulates Mainly One Angle; His Automatic Pilot Regulates a Three-Component Vector\n    ID: 20260921-ashby-bicycle-and-aircraft-do-not-have-the-same-control-dimensionality\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: Asking whether a human “has control” over two systems conceals whether one requires regulation of one important dimension and the other twenty interacting dimensions.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: Does the human-control problem become impossible at some dimensionality unless regulation is hierarchically delegated to lower-level automated controllers?\n", "operative-ekphrasis.txt": "MOC: [[operative-ekphrasis]]\nMembers: 1\n\n011. In Musicolour, Doing Nothing Did Not Leave the System in the Same State\n    ID: 20260921-musicolour-silence-changes-the-machine\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: Silence is not a neutral gap between moves. Waiting changes the game. The exact same sound played before and after a quiet interval can encounter a differently sensitized machine.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: Should time itself be represented as an operative component of a language-game when memory decay, caches, environment state, model updates, external data, or adaptive thresholds make delay consequential?\n", "possibility-space-of-prompting.txt": "MOC: [[possibility-space-of-prompting]]\nMembers: 1\n\n016. Solar-Lezama Notes That a Buggy Stencil Can Still Produce the Correct Answer—Only More Slowly\n    ID: 20260921-stencil-can-return-the-right-answer-for-the-wrong-computation\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: A verifier can certify the wrong thing if it observes only the final state. More verification does not necessarily improve correspondence; the tested variable must be the one that actually matters.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: Should prompt tests specify not only acceptable outcomes but acceptable paths, resource costs, dependencies, provenance, and state transitions?\n", "practice-before-procedure.txt": "MOC: [[practice-before-procedure]]\nMembers: 1\n\n007. Bolt’s System Could Become More Useful Without Making the Speech Recognizer More Accurate\n    ID: 20260921-put-that-there-effective-accuracy-includes-repairability\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: A better prompt interface need not make the model understand the first instruction more often. It can instead alter when errors become visible and how far their consequences propagate before correction.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: Can an interaction system with a less accurate model outperform a more accurate model because it externalizes misunderstandings earlier and supports cheaper correction?\n", "prompt-box.txt": "MOC: [[prompt-box]]\nMembers: 1\n\n002. Under Time Pressure, Engelbart’s Brick-Pencil Did Not Merely Slow Writing: It Made the Writing Larger\n    ID: 20260921-brick-pencil-changes-the-size-of-thoughts-on-paper\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: The medium’s limitation becomes visible in the morphology of the resulting language artifact. Large writing is not a “preference” for large writing; it is evidence of the control problem produced by the tool.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: Could prompt corpora be read archaeologically, with verbosity, headings, repetition, capitalization, delimiters, and defensive constraints treated as traces of the failures users were compensating for?\n", "prompt-comparison.txt": "MOC: [[prompt-comparison]]\nMembers: 1\n\n001. “Create a Blue Square There” Also Creates “Medium,” Even Though Nobody Says Medium\n    ID: 20260921-bolt-medium-is-executed-without-being-said\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: The final blue medium square looks like one unified response to one unified command, but “blue,” “square,” position, and size have four different provenance stories. Looking only at the sentence conceals which parts the user actually specified.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: Can a generated artifact be accompanied by a provenance map that distinguishes explicitly stated, gesturally supplied, contextually inferred, defaulted, retrieved, and autonomously chosen properties?\n", "prompt-harness.txt": "MOC: [[prompt-harness]]\nMembers: 1\n\n005. TeX 0.7 Is Historically Real Even Though Knuth Withdrew Both the Change and the Version\n    ID: 20260921-tex-version-zero-seven-existed-as-a-retracted-belief\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: Version 0.7 disappears from the accepted technical lineage but remains essential to the knowledge lineage. The important artifact is partly a belief transition that cannot be reconstructed from the surviving code alone.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: What should versioning mean when two configurations have identical active text but differ because one comes before a failed intervention and the other after its retraction?\n", "prompt-knowledge.txt": "MOC: [[prompt-knowledge]]\nMembers: 1\n\n010. Group B’s Documentation Already Described the Relevant Compiler Facilities, Yet Group B Still Reached for Patches\n    ID: 20260921-naur-documentation-contained-the-answer-but-not-the-seeing\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: The missing knowledge is not equivalent to a missing sentence. Group B could possess the proposition “facility F exists” without seeing that the new problem was a case for F.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: Can a harness preserve not only solutions and explanations but the similarity judgments that caused builders to treat two superficially different failures as instances of one mechanism?\n", "prompt-stabilization.txt": "MOC: [[prompt-stabilization]]\nMembers: 1\n\n008. A User Can Operate on a Computational Object While Lacking the Vocabulary Needed to Describe It\n    ID: 20260921-ostension-controls-an-object-without-possessing-its-name\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: Control does not require a shared descriptive vocabulary for the controlled object. A user can know which thing they mean without knowing what the system calls it.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: When a world is directly addressable, should natural language describe relations and intentions while pointing, selection, and object identity carry reference?\n", "prompting-as-language-game.txt": "MOC: [[prompting-as-language-game]]\nMembers: 1\n\n018. Patience Removes the Opponent; the Child’s Ball Removes Winning and Losing Too\n    ID: 20260921-wittgenstein-removes-competition-before-he-removes-winning\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: The useful method is not merely collecting weird edge cases. It is constructing a controlled path where each neighboring case removes one candidate essential property while preserving enough continuity to keep comparison intelligible.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: Can the five-term definition MOVE + GAME + SITUATION + EXECUTOR + TEST be pressure-tested through minimal pairs rather than through unrelated counterexamples?\n", "text-code-aspect.txt": "MOC: [[text-code-aspect]]\nMembers: 1\n\n003. In LISP the Inner Expression Can Stay Identical While One Outer Operator Prevents Its Evaluation\n    ID: 20260921-quote-changes-the-fate-of-the-insides\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: The decisive difference between inert representation and computation can sit outside the content whose “meaning” appears to be under discussion. The inner expression does not announce its own causal status.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: Could natural-language systems separate content from execution authority by attaching a typed outer mode that cannot itself be overridden by instructions inside the quoted material?\n", "working-definition-of-prompt.txt": "MOC: [[working-definition-of-prompt]]\nMembers: 1\n\n017. Put-That-There Tests Pointing Continuously but Reference Commitment Discretely\n    ID: 20260921-put-that-there-has-continuous-and-discrete-tests-before-action\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: There is no single “interpretation moment.” One layer is continuously visible before language arrives; another binding occurs when a deictic token is spoken; another consequence appears only after action.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: Can correspondence be made easier to debug by showing the exact instant at which ambiguous evidence becomes a binding that downstream operations must obey?\n", "worldtext.txt": "MOC: [[worldtext]]\nMembers: 1\n\n012. Sketchpad Could Make One Visible “Object” Half Instance and Half Copy\n    ID: 20260921-sketchpad-arrowhead-has-fixed-identity-and-flexible-tail\n    WHAT BECAME STRANGE: The question “is this the same object?” becomes too coarse even inside one arrow. The head can preserve instance identity while the tail remains structurally open to modification.\n    DEEPER QUESTION: Can a user say “keep the character the same but change the costume” only if the system represents identity at multiple nested scopes?\n"}, "arrangements": {"distributed-specification-commitment-repair.txt": "ARRANGEMENT: Distributed specification -> commitment -> repair\nStatus: DERIVED interpretation; not evidence.\n\n01. “Create a Blue Square There” Also Creates “Medium,” Even Though Nobody Says Medium  [[20260921-bolt-medium-is-executed-without-being-said]]\n02. A User Can Operate on a Computational Object While Lacking the Vocabulary Needed to Describe It  [[20260921-ostension-controls-an-object-without-possessing-its-name]]\n03. In LISP the Inner Expression Can Stay Identical While One Outer Operator Prevents Its Evaluation  [[20260921-quote-changes-the-fate-of-the-insides]]\n04. SHRDLU Did Not Silently Resolve “It”: It Announced the Binding Before Continuing  [[20260921-shrdlu-says-what-it-thinks-it-means-before-moving]]\n05. “Steeple” Becomes More Than a New Word Only Because Existing Spatial Relations Can Make One  [[20260921-shrdlu-new-noun-acquires-construction-consequences]]\n06. One of Sketchpad’s Copied Lines Can Disappear Immediately While Its Constraint Survives  [[20260921-sketchpad-copy-operation-installs-a-rule-that-you-never-see]]\n07. ReAct’s “Thought” Does Not Change the Environment, Yet It Changes the Context That Selects Later Actions  [[20260921-react-thought-is-externally-inert-but-future-causally-active]]\n08. Hans’s “Reading” Fell from 100 Percent to Zero When the Questioner Lost Positional Knowledge  [[20260921-clever-hans-word-reading-goes-from-all-correct-to-none]]\n09. Solar-Lezama Notes That a Buggy Stencil Can Still Produce the Correct Answer—Only More Slowly  [[20260921-stencil-can-return-the-right-answer-for-the-wrong-computation]]\n10. Bolt’s System Could Become More Useful Without Making the Speech Recognizer More Accurate  [[20260921-put-that-there-effective-accuracy-includes-repairability]]\n11. Ashby’s Bicycle Rider Regulates Mainly One Angle; His Automatic Pilot Regulates a Three-Component Vector  [[20260921-ashby-bicycle-and-aircraft-do-not-have-the-same-control-dimensionality]]\n12. Group B’s Documentation Already Described the Relevant Compiler Facilities, Yet Group B Still Reached for Patches  [[20260921-naur-documentation-contained-the-answer-but-not-the-seeing]]\n13. Patience Removes the Opponent; the Child’s Ball Removes Winning and Losing Too  [[20260921-wittgenstein-removes-competition-before-he-removes-winning]]\n"}, "paper": {"pdf_b64": "", "pdf_name": "prompt-does-not-contain-its-own-force__2026-09-21.pdf", "text": "Working Paper                     The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force                       1/13\n\n\n\n        The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force\n    Distributed Specification, Commitment, and Repair in Language-Controlled\n                                     Systems\n\n                                               Watson Hartsoe\n                              Digital Media, Georgia Institute of Technology\n\n                                    Working Paper · 21 September 2026\n\n\n                                                    Abstract\n\n          The prompt is usually treated as a piece of text supplied to a model. That unit\n      is too small for many of the systems now described as prompt-driven. A command\n      such as “Create a blue square there” can distribute its specification across speech,\n      gesture, defaults, persistent state, and an executor. The sentence alone does not\n      contain the location, the default size, the authority to act, or the test by which the\n      result becomes acceptable. This paper develops a different account from a set of\n      concrete technical cases: Bolt’s Put-That-There, McCarthy’s LISP evaluator, Winograd’s\n      SHRDLU, Sutherland’s Sketchpad, ReAct-style language agents, Pfungst’s Clever Hans\n      experiments, Ashby’s control theory, Naur’s theory-building account of programming,\n      and program sketching. Across these cases, three recurrent operations become\n      visible. Specification is distributed across channels and state. Operative force appears\n      only when a representation crosses a commitment boundary. Correspondence is\n      established through feedback and repair rather than by textual completeness alone.\n      The resulting claim is not that all prompting has one hidden essence. It is that prompt\n      research should stop attributing the work of the whole arrangement to the visible\n      string. The practical consequence is an instrumentation agenda: track parameter\n      provenance, expose consequential bindings before commitment, distinguish state\n      spaces, evaluate trajectories as well as terminal outputs, and treat repair cost as a\n      first-class property of prompt systems.\n\n\nKeywords: prompting; language-games; multimodal interfaces; execution; repair; dis-\ntributed specification; agent systems; human-computer interaction\n\n\n\n1 Introduction\n\nA blue square appears on a screen. The user has said, “Create a blue square there.” It is\ntempting to describe the resulting object as the execution of that sentence. Richard Bolt’s\nPut-That-There makes this description difficult to sustain. In Bolt’s system, the words sup-\nply the color and shape, the spoken “there” samples a pointing stream to supply a location,\nand the size can be supplied by a system default rather than by the user’s utterance [Bolt,\n1980]. The rendered square is one object, but its properties do not have one source. The\n\n\n\nWorking Paper · AI-augmented research process · Evidence, prompts, and making history preserved\n\fWorking Paper                     The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force                       2/13\n\n\n\nevent that produces it is already distributed across language, gesture, timing, interface\nstate, and a procedure that knows how to combine them.\n\nThis small case exposes a larger problem with the ordinary unit of prompt analysis. Cur-\nrent discussion often treats a prompt as an inscription whose quality can be judged by\ninspecting the words. Yet the words can be only one contribution to an operative arrange-\nment. A referent can be supplied by pointing rather than naming. A parameter can be\nsupplied by a default rather than by the user. A pronoun can depend on remembered\naction state. A string can remain inert or become executable depending on an evaluator\nor router outside the string. A model-generated “thought” can leave the external envi-\nronment unchanged while still altering the context from which a later action is chosen\n[McCarthy, 1960, Winograd, 1971, Yao et al., 2023]. These are not marginal complications.\nThey change what counts as the thing being studied.\n\nThe argument of this paper is that the operative unit of prompting is often a distributed\nevent rather than a text object. Three distinctions follow. First, specification has prove-\nnance: different properties of an outcome can be supplied by different channels, stored\nstates, defaults, or components. Second, interpretation and action are separated by com-\nmitment boundaries: a representation becomes consequential only when some part of a\nsystem binds, routes, authorizes, or executes it. Third, correctness is often interactional:\nusers discover what the system took them to mean through feedback, and effective per-\nformance depends on the cost and timing of repair as much as on first-pass recognition.\nThese distinctions do not define a timeless essence of “the prompt.” They provide a way\nto locate where the work happens in concrete systems.\n\nThe point is methodological as much as conceptual. When prompt research attributes\nsuccess or failure to the text alone, it can misidentify the cause. A successful output can\ndepend on a hidden default. A benchmark score can depend on information in the evalu-\nator’s side of an arrangement. A final answer can be correct even when the process that\nproduced it is defective. A supervisor can possess formal authority while lacking enough\nobservable dimensions and interventions to regulate the system in practice [Pfungst, 1911,\nSolar-Lezama et al., 2007, Ashby, 1956]. The research task is therefore to decompose the\napparent prompt-response pair into the state transitions that make it operative.\n\n\n\n2 From Prompt String to Distributed Specification\n\nBolt’s interface is useful because its machinery is explicit enough to make specification\nprovenance visible. The utterance “Create a blue square there” has the surface form of a\nsingle instruction, but the system does not obtain all of its arguments from that surface.\nBolt describes the graphical command in procedural terms: a creation routine receives\nattributes, and the deictic word “there” functions as a temporal signal that samples the\npointing cursor. The location is therefore not encoded in the lexical content of the word.\nThe user’s hand supplies a changing coordinate stream, and the speech event selects one\ninstant from it [Bolt, 1980]. In the same example, size can be omitted because the interface\n\n\nWorking Paper · AI-augmented research process · Evidence, prompts, and making history preserved\n\fWorking Paper                     The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force                       3/13\n\n\n\nsupplies a default value. Omission has several different meanings inside one command.\nSome information is mandatory in speech, some is supplied by gesture, and some is filled\nautomatically.\n\nThe familiar distinction between explicit and implicit prompting is too coarse for this case.\nA better question is: who or what supplied each operative value? For the square, color and\nshape can be tagged as language-supplied, position as gesture-supplied, and size as default-\nsupplied. In a contemporary agent system, the same provenance vocabulary would need\nto expand. A property might be stated by the user, recovered from conversation history,\nretrieved from a database, inherited from a template, supplied by a tool, defaulted by an\ninterface, inferred by a model, or chosen by an executor. These sources have different im-\nplications for attribution and repair. If an unwanted value came from a default, rewriting\nthe user’s sentence is an indirect fix. If it came from a retrieved state, the failure belongs\nelsewhere.\n\nThe same distributed structure appears in reference. Bolt’s user can act on an object by say-\ning “that” while pointing, without possessing or uttering the system’s name for the object.\nThe user can know which object is intended without being able to describe it proposition-\nally [Bolt, 1980]. This matters for generative interfaces because text boxes force two tasks\ninto one channel: the user must both identify a thing and state what relation or operation\nshould apply to it. A directly addressable world can separate those burdens. Pointing, se-\nlection, object identity, and spatial persistence can carry reference while language carries\nrelations, intentions, or constraints. In such a system, shorter language need not mean less\nspecification. The missing words may have been replaced by higher-bandwidth state and\ngesture.\n\nEngelbart’s brick-pencil thought experiment gives a historical reason to take these inter-\nface effects seriously. A tool can change the visible morphology of symbolic work. When\nthe writing instrument becomes physically awkward, users do not merely work more\nslowly; under pressure they enlarge their writing and alter their technique [Engelbart,\n1962]. Prompt style can be studied in the same way. Repetition, headings, delimiters, de-\nfensive restatement, and other conventions may sometimes be durable cognitive aids, but\nthey may also be compensatory forms produced by a particular model-interface relation.\nA history of prompt technique should therefore ask which linguistic forms survive when\nthe underlying friction disappears.\n\n\n\n3 Operative Force Is Assigned at a Commitment Boundary\n\nDistributed specification explains where inputs come from, but it does not yet explain\nwhen an inscription becomes operative. McCarthy’s LISP evaluator supplies a clean tech-\nnical contrast. The interior symbolic expression does not determine its own evaluation\nstatus. A surrounding form such as QUOTE changes what the evaluator does with the\nsame symbolic material. Under quotation, the expression is taken as itself rather than\nrecursively evaluated; under ordinary evaluation, the expression participates in compu-\n\n\nWorking Paper · AI-augmented research process · Evidence, prompts, and making history preserved\n\fWorking Paper                     The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force                       4/13\n\n\n\ntation [McCarthy, 1960]. The causal status of the expression depends on a relation between\nrepresentation and evaluator.\n\nNatural-language agent systems usually lack a single formal eval operation, but the dis-\ntinction survives in distributed form. Text can be displayed, stored, summarized, embed-\nded into memory, interpreted as a tool request, routed to an executor, or used to construct\nanother prompt. The same imperative sentence can therefore remain inert in one channel\nand produce external effects in another. ReAct makes this split explicit inside a language-\nagent architecture. It augments the environmental action space with a language space. A\nlanguage-space thought does not itself produce an external observation, but it enters the\ncontext that conditions subsequent reasoning and action [Yao et al., 2023]. “Inert” is there-\nfore relative to a state boundary. The thought is externally inert while remaining causally\nactive with respect to the next model state.\n\nThis suggests that a binary distinction between text and action is still too simple. A useful\ncausal inventory must name the state that changed. A generated span can modify conver-\nsational context without changing a file. It can update memory without invoking a tool.\nIt can cause a tool call without directly producing the tool’s external effect. It can alter\na human operator’s decision even if every machine state remains fixed. For agent instru-\nmentation, the relevant question is not simply whether the model “acted.” It is which layer\naccepted a representation as a commitment and which state spaces changed afterward.\n\nAn explicit commitment boundary also clarifies why plain-language containment is frag-\nile. Telling a model to “treat the following as data” is not equivalent to a language run-\ntime’s enforced quotation mode. Both the instruction that establishes the boundary and\nthe supposedly inert content can be visible to the same interpreter. The LISP example\nshows a different architecture: evaluation status is carried by syntax that the evaluator\nitself enforces. The corresponding design problem for language-controlled systems is to\nseparate content from execution authority in a channel that the content cannot redefine\nby persuasion alone. This is an architectural problem, not a prompt-wording trick.\n\n\n\n4 Stateful Reference, Constructive Definitions, and Persistent\n  Rules\n\nSHRDLU shows a second way in which the prompt string is too small. Winograd’s system\ndoes not interpret each sentence against an empty background. It uses discourse context,\nworld knowledge, a representation of its own actions, and a modeled scene in order to\nanswer questions and execute commands [Winograd, 1971]. In the canonical exchange\ninvolving a block taller than the one the robot is holding, the pronoun “it” can be resolved\nonly because the system maintains information about prior action and the current world.\nPart of what appears to be linguistic competence is therefore state bookkeeping outside\nthe current sentence.\n\nMore importantly, SHRDLU can expose some of that bookkeeping before acting. In the\n\n\nWorking Paper · AI-augmented research process · Evidence, prompts, and making history preserved\n\fWorking Paper                     The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force                       5/13\n\n\n\ndocumented dialogue, the system explicitly states what it assumes “it” refers to before\nproceeding. This is a small but consequential interface choice. The user does not have\nto infer reference resolution only from the final movement of a block. A fragile binding\nbecomes an inspectable assertion before execution. The design principle is not that every\ninterpretation should be narrated. Constant confirmation would be unusable. The more\nspecific possibility is consequence-sensitive exposure: surface a binding when plausible\nalternatives would lead to materially different actions.\n\nSHRDLU also complicates the idea that a new prompt concept corresponds to a new capa-\nbility. A term such as “steeple” can be defined through relations already available in the\nblocks world and then used in later reasoning and construction. The new noun becomes\noperative because it composes an existing vocabulary of shapes, sizes, spatial relations,\nsearch procedures, and manipulation actions [Winograd, 1971]. A user-defined concept\ncan therefore reorganize capabilities that were already executable. The question “did the\nprompt add a capability?” is underspecified unless one distinguishes a genuinely new\nprimitive from a new composition of existing operations.\n\nSutherland’s Sketchpad makes persistence even more concrete. A graphical operation can\ninstall a relation whose most important consequence is not visible in the immediate frame.\nSutherland describes constraints, attachers, copies, and instances through which a rela-\ntion such as equal length can be copied and merged into other graphical elements. The\nscaffolding used to establish the constraint can disappear while the constraint continues to\ngovern future changes [Sutherland, 1963]. The visible delta can be small while the causal\ndelta is large. A one-time edit and a persistent rule can therefore produce the same screen-\nshot and very different futures.\n\nSketchpad’s distinction between instances and copies also shows why visual continuity is\nnot enough for persistent worlds. Two shapes can look the same while supporting differ-\nent future operations. A component can preserve a fixed internal identity while another\ncomponent remains structurally flexible. Identity is therefore not one Boolean property\nof the rendered object. It can attach at different scopes: whole object, component, relation,\nrole, or provenance. Language such as “keep the same character but change the costume”\npresupposes some such layered identity, whether it is represented symbolically or recon-\nstructed by another mechanism. A persistent world must preserve not only appearance\nbut enough addressable structure for later references and transformations to have stable\ntargets.\n\n\n\n5 Tests and Evaluators Are Part of the Effective Input\n\nThe same distributed analysis applies to evaluation. Pfungst’s Clever Hans experiments\nremain useful because they show how information can enter a task from the evaluator’s\nside of an arrangement without anyone intending to send it. In word-reading trials, Hans\nperformed perfectly when the questioner knew the requested placard’s position and failed\nwhen the questioner lacked that positional knowledge [Pfungst, 1911]. The nominal vis-\n\n\nWorking Paper · AI-augmented research process · Evidence, prompts, and making history preserved\n\fWorking Paper                     The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force                       6/13\n\n\n\nible task remained a reading task. What changed was a state variable in another partic-\nipant. The result demonstrated that the effective input included information carried by\nthe experimental arrangement.\n\nFor model evaluation, the corresponding question is not whether the grader is honest\nor blinded in an informal sense. It is which components possess answer-relevant infor-\nmation and which channels can transmit it before commitment. A model can obtain\ntarget-correlated evidence from task wrappers, metadata, repeated grader responses, re-\ntrieval tools, benchmark conventions, or prior exposure. Capability attribution therefore\nrequires interventions over the whole information topology. A benchmark can only iso-\nlate a model property if the alternative channels that could produce the observed perfor-\nmance have been controlled or at least measured.\n\nProgram synthesis introduces another evaluation problem. Solar-Lezama and colleagues\nnote that a buggy iterative stencil can still converge to the correct final answer while taking\nmuch longer to do so [Solar-Lezama et al., 2007]. A terminal-output verifier can therefore\naccept an implementation whose process is defective. This is directly relevant to prompt\nsystems with agents and tools. If the test observes only the final artifact, it can miss illegal\nintermediate states, wasted resource use, brittle dependencies, repeated retries, or policy\nviolations. More verification is not automatically better verification. The verifier must\nobserve variables that correspond to the intended property.\n\nThe tension is real because process constraints can also become over-specification. A ver-\nifier that dictates every intermediate step prevents alternative valid procedures and de-\nfeats part of the value of generative search. The design task is to separate invariants from\nopen implementation choices. In a building system, attachment validity or collision con-\nstraints may be true invariants while construction sequence remains open. In an agent,\nauthorization boundaries may be invariant while the internal plan remains flexible. The\ntest should constrain what must remain true, not recreate a full hand-written solution\nunless the task actually requires one.\n\n\n\n6 Repair Changes the Meaning of Accuracy\n\nBolt’s later description of Put-That-There explicitly begins from fallibility. The project as-\nsumes that speech recognition will not be perfectly accurate and pursues greater “effective\naccuracy” through redundant channels, context-sensitive interpretation, early feedback,\nand easy correction [Bolt et al., 1982]. This reframes the engineering problem. A system\ncan become more useful without improving the recognizer if it reduces the distance be-\ntween a wrong interpretation and the moment at which the user can detect and correct\nit.\n\nThe interface provides several kinds of feedback at different temporal scales. Pointing can\nbe shown continuously as a cursor. A spoken deictic such as “there” commits a sampled lo-\ncation at a particular moment. An addressed graphical item can receive immediate visual\n\n\nWorking Paper · AI-augmented research process · Evidence, prompts, and making history preserved\n\fWorking Paper                     The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force                       7/13\n\n\n\nacknowledgment before the larger action completes [Bolt, 1980]. There is no single inter-\npretation moment. Evidence accumulates continuously, some bindings become discrete\ncommitments, and the final world change occurs later. A useful trace should preserve\nthese distinctions rather than collapsing the whole sequence into one prompt and one\nresponse.\n\nThis produces a different criterion for prompt-interface quality. First-pass task success\nremains important, but it is incomplete. Systems should also be evaluated by detection\nlatency, repair distance, correction effort, and propagation cost. A weaker model with\nvisible provisional bindings and cheap repair can outperform a stronger opaque model\non completed-task correctness. That is an empirical claim to be tested, not assumed, but\nBolt’s architecture shows that such a comparison is coherent. It moves research away\nfrom the premise that all improvement must come from better linguistic instruction or a\nmore capable recognizer.\n\nRepair also changes the status of a “test.” A system need not wait until the end of a gen-\neration to compare output with intent. Referential highlighting, previews, selected tool\ndisplays, constraint visualizations, and snap cues can function as micro-tests of interme-\ndiate commitments. Each one turns a hidden interpretation into an inspectable state. The\nappropriate interface need not interrupt the user with a question at every step. It can\nexpose enough state for the user to notice divergence before errors propagate.\n\n\n\n7 Control Is Not a Scalar Possession\n\nAshby’s account of regulation gives a precise reason why prompt specificity should not be\nconfused with control. His automatic-pilot example treats yaw, pitch, and roll as distinct\nessential variables that must remain within acceptable limits; his bicycle example centers\non a different essential variable, the rider’s angle with the vertical [Ashby, 1956]. Control\ndepends on what dimensions must be regulated, what disturbances affect them, what can\nbe observed, and what responses are available. It is not a single quantity that one either\npossesses or lacks.\n\nA human operator can therefore have formal authority without having adequate regula-\ntory capacity. A stop button can terminate a system while providing almost no ability to\nsteer ordinary behavior. A text prompt can express a high-level preference while expos-\ning none of the intermediate variables required to diagnose a failure. In complex agent\nsystems, “human control” should be decomposed into observable essential variables and\navailable intervention channels. The dimensions should be discovered in relation to a\nconcrete deployment rather than supplied as a generic list.\n\nThis is where distributed specification and repair return to control theory. Direct manipu-\nlation can move reference work out of prose. Constraint visualizations can expose persis-\ntent rules. Commitment previews can reveal selected tools or targets. Process-aware tests\ncan make previously hidden deviations observable. These interface structures increase\n\n\nWorking Paper · AI-augmented research process · Evidence, prompts, and making history preserved\n\fWorking Paper                     The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force                       8/13\n\n\n\nthe regulator’s available distinctions and interventions. They do not guarantee control,\nbut they alter the variety of situations to which the operator can respond. The design of\na prompt interface is therefore partly the design of a regulator’s observation and action\nrepertoire.\n\n\n\n8 Why More Documentation Is Not Enough\n\nInstrumentation can still fail if it is treated as a substitute for understanding. Naur’s ac-\ncount of programming as theory building describes a compiler-maintenance case in which\na later group possessed extensive program text and documentation but did not recognize\nthat an existing facility applied to a new requirement. The original programmers saw a\nsimpler modification because they possessed a theory of how the system’s parts related\nto its domain [Naur, 1985]. The missing resource was not merely another sentence in the\nmanual. It was a discriminating judgment about which difference was relevant.\n\nPrompt systems invite the same failure mode. A team can accumulate logs, retries, excep-\ntion clauses, and successful patches while losing the abstraction that made the system easy\nto modify. Two changes can pass the same local test while only one preserves a reusable ac-\ncount of the mechanism. A useful harness should therefore preserve cases and rationales\nin a form that supports comparison across superficially different failures. The strongest\nevidence of theory is not successful repetition of a known recipe but coherent modifica-\ntion under a novel change.\n\nThe retracted TeX 0.7 episode preserved in Knuth’s error log makes the complementary\npoint. A configuration can return to an earlier textual state after evidence overturns an\nintervening change, yet the knowledge state of the project has changed. The same active\ntext before and after retraction does not imply the same theory of why the text is correct\n[Knuth, 1992]. For prompt harnesses, deleted clauses and failed fixes can be valuable\nnegative knowledge. Versioning should be able to preserve the evidence that introduced\nand later killed a rule, rather than storing only the currently active prompt.\n\n\n\n9 Prompt as a Family of Operative Arrangements\n\nThese cases do not imply that every event called prompting has the same hidden archi-\ntecture. Wittgenstein’s discussion of games provides a better methodological model. He\nmoves from board games to card games, patience, ball games, and the child throwing a\nball against a wall, watching candidate common properties appear and disappear rather\nthan insisting on one feature present in every case [Wittgenstein, 1953]. The useful oper-\nation is controlled comparison between neighbors.\n\nPrompt research can use the same strategy. Begin with a paradigmatic agentic natural-\nlanguage prompt and remove one feature at a time. Remove the explicit verifier while\n\n\nWorking Paper · AI-augmented research process · Evidence, prompts, and making history preserved\n\fWorking Paper                     The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force                       9/13\n\n\n\nholding the executor fixed. Replace text with gesture while preserving the operation. Re-\nplace a stochastic model with a deterministic program. Remove persistent world state.\nRemove the user-authored move and let an agent initiate the action. At each transition,\nask what explanatory work the word “prompt” still performs. This approach can reveal\noverlapping operational families without turning family resemblance into an excuse for\nvagueness.\n\nThe result also limits the thesis of this paper. Distributed specification, commitment\nboundaries, and repair are not proposed as necessary and sufficient conditions for every\nprompt. They are recurring structures that become visible in historically and technically\nspecific systems. Some interfaces will have no gesture channel. Some operations will\nhave no persistent world. Some model calls will be genuinely one-shot. The claim is\nnarrower: when those structures are present, treating the visible string as the complete\ncausal or semantic unit discards information needed to explain the result.\n\n\n\n10 An Instrumentation Agenda\n\nA practical research program follows from the distinctions above. First, systems should\nrecord parameter provenance where it can be represented without pretending to more\nprecision than the architecture supports. If a value came from a user utterance, a pointer\nsample, a default, a retrieved record, a persistent object identity, or an executor policy,\nthe trace should distinguish those sources. For neural generation, provenance will often\nbe coarser than a constructor’s argument list, but even coarse provenance is more infor-\nmative than attributing every property to the prompt text.\n\nSecond, interfaces should distinguish evidence from commitment. Continuously chang-\ning evidence can remain provisional; a tool selection, object binding, coordinate sample,\npermission grant, or transaction commit can be logged as a discrete state transition. Conse-\nquential bindings should be exposed before execution when plausible alternatives would\nlead to materially different effects. This is not a demand for constant confirmation. It is a\ndemand that the architecture know where commitment occurs and give the interface the\noption to surface it.\n\nThird, traces should identify the state space affected by every operation. ReAct’s\nthought/action distinction is one example, but a contemporary agent may also update\nmemory, retrieval state, tool state, file state, environment state, or human decision state.\n“No action” is meaningful only relative to one of these spaces. A causal-state inventory can\nprevent a system from labeling an operation inert simply because it did not immediately\ntouch the external environment.\n\nFourth, tests should be attached to the property being claimed. Terminal output is ap-\npropriate for some tasks, but it is insufficient when trajectory, resource use, authoriza-\ntion, persistent constraints, or provenance are part of the specification. Conversely, tests\nshould not unnecessarily freeze implementation choices that should remain open. The\n\n\nWorking Paper · AI-augmented research process · Evidence, prompts, and making history preserved\n\fWorking Paper                     The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force                       10/13\n\n\n\nrelevant distinction is between invariants and degrees of freedom, not between strict and\nloose prompting.\n\nFifth, repair should be measured. First-pass success obscures interaction systems whose\nmistakes are cheap to see and correct, as well as systems whose rare mistakes propagate\ncatastrophically before they become visible. Detection latency, repair distance, correction\nburden, and residual state after correction are all observable quantities. These measures\nmove prompt evaluation closer to the actual work of operating a system over time.\n\nFinally, archives should preserve failed theories as well as successful configurations.\nNaur’s maintenance case and Knuth’s retracted version both show why a final artifact\ncannot carry the complete history of the judgments that produced it. Prompt harnesses\nthat record only the latest system message will repeatedly rediscover old mistakes. A\nuseful research record should preserve the failure that motivated a change, the mech-\nanism the change was supposed to address, the evidence that supported it, and the\ncounterevidence that later invalidated it.\n\n\n\n11 Limitations and Unresolved Territory\n\nThe cases assembled here differ sharply in scale and mechanism. Sketchpad and LISP\nexpose deterministic structures that contemporary neural systems often hide. SHRDLU\nworks in a deliberately tiny symbolic world. Clever Hans concerns perceptible cues in an\nanimal experiment. Ashby’s variables are analytical abstractions, not ready-made metrics\nfor AI governance. ReAct describes a research architecture rather than every deployed\nagent. These differences block any simple historical claim that modern prompting directly\ndescends from one of these systems. The comparisons are functional and diagnostic unless\ndocumentary evidence establishes a genealogy.\n\nThe central unresolved technical problem is representation. Parameter provenance is\neasy when a constructor has explicit arguments and difficult when a generated property\nemerges from distributed latent computation. Commitment is easy to mark when a trans-\naction has a clear commit operation and harder when model output incrementally affects\ndownstream processes. Persistent identity is explicit in Sketchpad and often heuristic in\ngenerated images or worlds. The paper therefore proposes instrumentation targets with-\nout assuming that every target has a clean symbolic implementation.\n\nA second unresolved problem is the boundary between helpful exposure and interface\noverload. Making every dependency visible can produce an unusable display. Asking for\nconfirmation at every ambiguity can destroy fluent interaction. Process-aware verifica-\ntion can overconstrain search. History-sensitive feedback can make a system difficult to\nlearn. The design question is selective exposure: which hidden states have sufficiently\ndifferent downstream consequences that the user should see them before commitment?\n\nA third problem concerns the term “prompt” itself. If future systems move reference into\nselection, constraints into direct manipulation, evaluation into executable tests, and del-\n\nWorking Paper · AI-augmented research process · Evidence, prompts, and making history preserved\n\fWorking Paper                     The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force                       11/13\n\n\n\negation into persistent agents, the visible prompt may shrink while the surrounding op-\nerative arrangement becomes richer. The category could stabilize around high-level un-\nderspecified instruction, or it could dissolve into a family of specialized controls. The\nappropriate research response is not to protect the word. It is to preserve the distinctions\nneeded to explain how descriptions become operations in each concrete practice.\n\n\n\n12 Conclusion\n\nThe sentence is often the most visible part of a prompt system, but visibility is not causal\nsufficiency. Bolt’s square receives properties from speech, gesture, and defaults. LISP sep-\narates symbolic content from evaluation status. SHRDLU resolves reference against per-\nsistent world and discourse state and can make a binding explicit before action. Sketchpad\ninstalls rules that survive beyond the visible edit. ReAct separates changes to context from\nchanges to environment. Pfungst shows that information on the evaluator’s side can be-\ncome effective input. Solar-Lezama shows that a correct terminal answer can conceal a\ndefective process. Ashby shows why control depends on the dimensions that can be ob-\nserved and regulated. Naur shows that even extensive traces and documentation do not\nautomatically yield the theory needed for modification.\n\nTaken together, these cases support a concrete change in research practice. Stop asking\nonly what words were sent to the model. Ask where each operative value came from,\nwhen an interpretation became a commitment, which state changed, what feedback ex-\nposed the change, what test evaluated it, and how far an error traveled before repair. That\nexpanded trace does not eliminate the prompt. It places the prompt inside the language-\ngame that gives it force.\n\n\n\nAcknowledgment of Process\n\nThis working paper was assembled in an AI-augmented research process from preserved\nzettels and cited sources. The immutable zettel payloads, prompts, source map, making\nhistory, and rebuild path are preserved in the accompanying SLIPCASE package. No hu-\nman scholarly review is claimed for this draft.\n\n\n\nReferences\n\nW. Ross Ashby. An Introduction to Cybernetics. Chapman & Hall, London, 1956.\n\nRichard A. Bolt. “Put-that-there”: Voice and Gesture at the Graphics Interface. In Proceed-\n  ings of SIGGRAPH 1980, pages 262–270. ACM, 1980. doi: 10.1145/800250.807503.\n\n\n\nWorking Paper · AI-augmented research process · Evidence, prompts, and making history preserved\n\fWorking Paper                     The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force                       12/13\n\n\n\nRichard A. Bolt, Chris Schmandt, and Eric A. Hulteen. Put-that-there: Voice and gesture at\n  the graphics interface. 1982. Architecture Machine Group, Massachusetts Institute of\n  Technology.\n\nDouglas C. Engelbart. Augmenting human intellect: A conceptual framework. Technical\n Report AFOSR-3223, Stanford Research Institute, 1962.\n\nDonald E. Knuth. Literate Programming. Center for the Study of Language and Informa-\n tion, 1992.\n\nJohn McCarthy. Recursive functions of symbolic expressions and their computation by\n  machine, part i. Communications of the ACM, 3(4):184–195, 1960.\n\nPeter Naur. Programming as theory building. Microprocessing and Microprogramming, 15:\n  253–261, 1985.\n\nOskar Pfungst. Clever Hans (The Horse of Mr. von Osten): A Contribution to Experimental\n Animal and Human Psychology. Henry Holt and Company, New York, 1911.\n\nArmando Solar-Lezama, Gilad Arnold, Liviu Tancau, Rastislav Bodik, Vijay Saraswat, and\n  Sanjit Seshia. Sketching stencils. In Proceedings of the ACM SIGPLAN Conference on\n  Programming Language Design and Implementation, 2007.\n\nIvan Edward Sutherland. Sketchpad: A Man-Machine Graphical Communication System.\n  PhD thesis, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, 1963.\n\nTerry Winograd. Procedures as a representation for data in a computer program for un-\n  derstanding natural language. Technical Report AI-TR-235, MIT Artificial Intelligence\n  Laboratory, 1971.\n\nLudwig Wittgenstein. Philosophical Investigations. Basil Blackwell, Oxford, 1953.\n\nShunyu Yao, Jeffrey Zhao, Dian Yu, Nan Du, Izhak Shafran, Karthik Narasimhan, and Yuan\n  Cao. React: Synergizing reasoning and acting in language models. In International Con-\n  ference on Learning Representations, 2023.\n\n\n\n\nA Assembly Instrument\n\nThe exact publication assembly prompt is preserved at _PROMPTS/SLIPCASE_ASSEMBLY_\nPROMPT_15_55_AM.txt in the accompanying package and embedded in index.html.\nThe full prompt is omitted here only to keep the working paper readable.\n\n\n\n\nWorking Paper · AI-augmented research process · Evidence, prompts, and making history preserved\n\fWorking Paper                     The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force                       13/13\n\n\n\nB Making History\n\nPROVIDED: the research prompts and 41-page proposal deck. PRESERVED: the admitted\nimmutable zettel payloads, prompt bodies, and local uploaded PDF. RETRIEVED: public\nsource pages used for source verification during assembly. DERIVED: this paper, source\nmap, bibliography views, graph, MOCs, arrangements, network, reader, mark, and veri-\nfication report. VERIFIED: deterministic hashes and structural counts; PDF compilation\nand rendering are recorded in the package verification report. UNVERIFIED: complete\nconversation coverage and human scholarly review.\n\n\n\nC Replication Path\n\nOpen index.html offline to inspect the admitted cards, sources, bibliography, prompts,\nghosts, and paper metadata. Use 000__RETURN_PATH.txt to rejoin the checkpoint. Use\nZETTELS.json and _SLIPCASE/reconstruct_cards.py to reconstruct immutable card\nfiles and verify their SHA-256 identities. Full limitations are recorded in _SLIPCASE/\nVERIFICATION.txt.\n\n\n\n\nWorking Paper · AI-augmented research process · Evidence, prompts, and making history preserved\n\f", "tex": "\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[margin=1.05in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{fontspec}\n\\setmainfont{Noto Serif}\n\\setsansfont{Inter}\n\\setmonofont{DejaVu Sans Mono}\n\\usepackage{microtype}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage[hidelinks]{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{fancyhdr}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{enumitem}\n\\usepackage{titlesec}\n\\usepackage{setspace}\n\\usepackage{lastpage}\n\\setlength{\\parindent}{0pt}\n\\setlength{\\parskip}{0.75em}\n\\setstretch{1.06}\n\\setlength{\\emergencystretch}{3em}\n\\titleformat{\\section}{\\Large\\bfseries}{\\thesection}{0.6em}{}\n\\titleformat{\\subsection}{\\large\\bfseries}{\\thesubsection}{0.6em}{}\n\\pagestyle{fancy}\n\\fancyhf{}\n\\lhead{\\footnotesize Working Paper}\n\\chead{\\footnotesize The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force}\n\\rhead{\\footnotesize \\thepage/\\pageref{LastPage}}\n\\fancyfoot[R]{\\includegraphics[width=0.34in]{MARK.pdf}}\n\\fancyfoot[L]{\\scriptsize Working Paper · AI-augmented research process · Evidence, prompts, and making history preserved}\n\\renewcommand{\\headrulewidth}{0.2pt}\n\\renewcommand{\\footrulewidth}{0pt}\n\\hypersetup{pdftitle={The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force},pdfauthor={Watson Hartsoe}}\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\begin{center}\n{\\LARGE\\bfseries The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force\\par}\n\\vspace{0.35em}\n{\\large Distributed Specification, Commitment, and Repair in Language-Controlled Systems\\par}\n\\vspace{1em}\n{\\normalsize Watson Hartsoe\\par}\n{\\small Digital Media, Georgia Institute of Technology\\par}\n\\vspace{0.7em}\n{\\small Working Paper · 21 September 2026\\par}\n\\end{center}\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThe prompt is usually treated as a piece of text supplied to a model. That unit is too small for many of the systems now described as prompt-driven. A command such as ``Create a blue square there'' can distribute its specification across speech, gesture, defaults, persistent state, and an executor. The sentence alone does not contain the location, the default size, the authority to act, or the test by which the result becomes acceptable. This paper develops a different account from a set of concrete technical cases: Bolt's \\emph{Put-That-There}, McCarthy's LISP evaluator, Winograd's SHRDLU, Sutherland's Sketchpad, ReAct-style language agents, Pfungst's Clever Hans experiments, Ashby's control theory, Naur's theory-building account of programming, and program sketching. Across these cases, three recurrent operations become visible. Specification is distributed across channels and state. Operative force appears only when a representation crosses a commitment boundary. Correspondence is established through feedback and repair rather than by textual completeness alone. The resulting claim is not that all prompting has one hidden essence. It is that prompt research should stop attributing the work of the whole arrangement to the visible string. The practical consequence is an instrumentation agenda: track parameter provenance, expose consequential bindings before commitment, distinguish state spaces, evaluate trajectories as well as terminal outputs, and treat repair cost as a first-class property of prompt systems.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\textbf{Keywords:} prompting; language-games; multimodal interfaces; execution; repair; distributed specification; agent systems; human-computer interaction\n\n\\section{Introduction}\n\n% CLAIM C01\nA blue square appears on a screen. The user has said, ``Create a blue square there.'' It is tempting to describe the resulting object as the execution of that sentence. Richard Bolt's \\emph{Put-That-There} makes this description difficult to sustain. In Bolt's system, the words supply the color and shape, the spoken ``there'' samples a pointing stream to supply a location, and the size can be supplied by a system default rather than by the user's utterance \\citep{bolt1980put}. The rendered square is one object, but its properties do not have one source. The event that produces it is already distributed across language, gesture, timing, interface state, and a procedure that knows how to combine them.\n\n% CLAIM C02\nThis small case exposes a larger problem with the ordinary unit of prompt analysis. Current discussion often treats a prompt as an inscription whose quality can be judged by inspecting the words. Yet the words can be only one contribution to an operative arrangement. A referent can be supplied by pointing rather than naming. A parameter can be supplied by a default rather than by the user. A pronoun can depend on remembered action state. A string can remain inert or become executable depending on an evaluator or router outside the string. A model-generated ``thought'' can leave the external environment unchanged while still altering the context from which a later action is chosen \\citep{mccarthy1960recursive,winograd1971procedures,yao2023react}. These are not marginal complications. They change what counts as the thing being studied.\n\n% CLAIM C03\nThe argument of this paper is that the operative unit of prompting is often a distributed event rather than a text object. Three distinctions follow. First, specification has provenance: different properties of an outcome can be supplied by different channels, stored states, defaults, or components. Second, interpretation and action are separated by commitment boundaries: a representation becomes consequential only when some part of a system binds, routes, authorizes, or executes it. Third, correctness is often interactional: users discover what the system took them to mean through feedback, and effective performance depends on the cost and timing of repair as much as on first-pass recognition. These distinctions do not define a timeless essence of ``the prompt.'' They provide a way to locate where the work happens in concrete systems.\n\n% CLAIM C04\nThe point is methodological as much as conceptual. When prompt research attributes success or failure to the text alone, it can misidentify the cause. A successful output can depend on a hidden default. A benchmark score can depend on information in the evaluator's side of an arrangement. A final answer can be correct even when the process that produced it is defective. A supervisor can possess formal authority while lacking enough observable dimensions and interventions to regulate the system in practice \\citep{pfungst1911clever,solarlezama2007sketching,ashby1956introduction}. The research task is therefore to decompose the apparent prompt-response pair into the state transitions that make it operative.\n\n\\section{From Prompt String to Distributed Specification}\n\n% CLAIM C05\nBolt's interface is useful because its machinery is explicit enough to make specification provenance visible. The utterance ``Create a blue square there'' has the surface form of a single instruction, but the system does not obtain all of its arguments from that surface. Bolt describes the graphical command in procedural terms: a creation routine receives attributes, and the deictic word ``there'' functions as a temporal signal that samples the pointing cursor. The location is therefore not encoded in the lexical content of the word. The user's hand supplies a changing coordinate stream, and the speech event selects one instant from it \\citep{bolt1980put}. In the same example, size can be omitted because the interface supplies a default value. Omission has several different meanings inside one command. Some information is mandatory in speech, some is supplied by gesture, and some is filled automatically.\n\n% CLAIM C06\nThe familiar distinction between explicit and implicit prompting is too coarse for this case. A better question is: who or what supplied each operative value? For the square, color and shape can be tagged as language-supplied, position as gesture-supplied, and size as default-supplied. In a contemporary agent system, the same provenance vocabulary would need to expand. A property might be stated by the user, recovered from conversation history, retrieved from a database, inherited from a template, supplied by a tool, defaulted by an interface, inferred by a model, or chosen by an executor. These sources have different implications for attribution and repair. If an unwanted value came from a default, rewriting the user's sentence is an indirect fix. If it came from a retrieved state, the failure belongs elsewhere.\n\n% CLAIM C07\nThe same distributed structure appears in reference. Bolt's user can act on an object by saying ``that'' while pointing, without possessing or uttering the system's name for the object. The user can know which object is intended without being able to describe it propositionally \\citep{bolt1980put}. This matters for generative interfaces because text boxes force two tasks into one channel: the user must both identify a thing and state what relation or operation should apply to it. A directly addressable world can separate those burdens. Pointing, selection, object identity, and spatial persistence can carry reference while language carries relations, intentions, or constraints. In such a system, shorter language need not mean less specification. The missing words may have been replaced by higher-bandwidth state and gesture.\n\n% CLAIM C08\nEngelbart's brick-pencil thought experiment gives a historical reason to take these interface effects seriously. A tool can change the visible morphology of symbolic work. When the writing instrument becomes physically awkward, users do not merely work more slowly; under pressure they enlarge their writing and alter their technique \\citep{engelbart1962augmenting}. Prompt style can be studied in the same way. Repetition, headings, delimiters, defensive restatement, and other conventions may sometimes be durable cognitive aids, but they may also be compensatory forms produced by a particular model-interface relation. A history of prompt technique should therefore ask which linguistic forms survive when the underlying friction disappears.\n\n\\section{Operative Force Is Assigned at a Commitment Boundary}\n\n% CLAIM C09\nDistributed specification explains where inputs come from, but it does not yet explain when an inscription becomes operative. McCarthy's LISP evaluator supplies a clean technical contrast. The interior symbolic expression does not determine its own evaluation status. A surrounding form such as QUOTE changes what the evaluator does with the same symbolic material. Under quotation, the expression is taken as itself rather than recursively evaluated; under ordinary evaluation, the expression participates in computation \\citep{mccarthy1960recursive}. The causal status of the expression depends on a relation between representation and evaluator.\n\n% CLAIM C10\nNatural-language agent systems usually lack a single formal \\texttt{eval} operation, but the distinction survives in distributed form. Text can be displayed, stored, summarized, embedded into memory, interpreted as a tool request, routed to an executor, or used to construct another prompt. The same imperative sentence can therefore remain inert in one channel and produce external effects in another. ReAct makes this split explicit inside a language-agent architecture. It augments the environmental action space with a language space. A language-space thought does not itself produce an external observation, but it enters the context that conditions subsequent reasoning and action \\citep{yao2023react}. ``Inert'' is therefore relative to a state boundary. The thought is externally inert while remaining causally active with respect to the next model state.\n\n% CLAIM C11\nThis suggests that a binary distinction between text and action is still too simple. A useful causal inventory must name the state that changed. A generated span can modify conversational context without changing a file. It can update memory without invoking a tool. It can cause a tool call without directly producing the tool's external effect. It can alter a human operator's decision even if every machine state remains fixed. For agent instrumentation, the relevant question is not simply whether the model ``acted.'' It is which layer accepted a representation as a commitment and which state spaces changed afterward.\n\n% CLAIM C12\nAn explicit commitment boundary also clarifies why plain-language containment is fragile. Telling a model to ``treat the following as data'' is not equivalent to a language runtime's enforced quotation mode. Both the instruction that establishes the boundary and the supposedly inert content can be visible to the same interpreter. The LISP example shows a different architecture: evaluation status is carried by syntax that the evaluator itself enforces. The corresponding design problem for language-controlled systems is to separate content from execution authority in a channel that the content cannot redefine by persuasion alone. This is an architectural problem, not a prompt-wording trick.\n\n\\section{Stateful Reference, Constructive Definitions, and Persistent Rules}\n\n% CLAIM C13\nSHRDLU shows a second way in which the prompt string is too small. Winograd's system does not interpret each sentence against an empty background. It uses discourse context, world knowledge, a representation of its own actions, and a modeled scene in order to answer questions and execute commands \\citep{winograd1971procedures}. In the canonical exchange involving a block taller than the one the robot is holding, the pronoun ``it'' can be resolved only because the system maintains information about prior action and the current world. Part of what appears to be linguistic competence is therefore state bookkeeping outside the current sentence.\n\n% CLAIM C14\nMore importantly, SHRDLU can expose some of that bookkeeping before acting. In the documented dialogue, the system explicitly states what it assumes ``it'' refers to before proceeding. This is a small but consequential interface choice. The user does not have to infer reference resolution only from the final movement of a block. A fragile binding becomes an inspectable assertion before execution. The design principle is not that every interpretation should be narrated. Constant confirmation would be unusable. The more specific possibility is consequence-sensitive exposure: surface a binding when plausible alternatives would lead to materially different actions.\n\n% CLAIM C15\nSHRDLU also complicates the idea that a new prompt concept corresponds to a new capability. A term such as ``steeple'' can be defined through relations already available in the blocks world and then used in later reasoning and construction. The new noun becomes operative because it composes an existing vocabulary of shapes, sizes, spatial relations, search procedures, and manipulation actions \\citep{winograd1971procedures}. A user-defined concept can therefore reorganize capabilities that were already executable. The question ``did the prompt add a capability?'' is underspecified unless one distinguishes a genuinely new primitive from a new composition of existing operations.\n\n% CLAIM C16\nSutherland's Sketchpad makes persistence even more concrete. A graphical operation can install a relation whose most important consequence is not visible in the immediate frame. Sutherland describes constraints, attachers, copies, and instances through which a relation such as equal length can be copied and merged into other graphical elements. The scaffolding used to establish the constraint can disappear while the constraint continues to govern future changes \\citep{sutherland1963sketchpad}. The visible delta can be small while the causal delta is large. A one-time edit and a persistent rule can therefore produce the same screenshot and very different futures.\n\n% CLAIM C17\nSketchpad's distinction between instances and copies also shows why visual continuity is not enough for persistent worlds. Two shapes can look the same while supporting different future operations. A component can preserve a fixed internal identity while another component remains structurally flexible. Identity is therefore not one Boolean property of the rendered object. It can attach at different scopes: whole object, component, relation, role, or provenance. Language such as ``keep the same character but change the costume'' presupposes some such layered identity, whether it is represented symbolically or reconstructed by another mechanism. A persistent world must preserve not only appearance but enough addressable structure for later references and transformations to have stable targets.\n\n\\section{Tests and Evaluators Are Part of the Effective Input}\n\n% CLAIM C18\nThe same distributed analysis applies to evaluation. Pfungst's Clever Hans experiments remain useful because they show how information can enter a task from the evaluator's side of an arrangement without anyone intending to send it. In word-reading trials, Hans performed perfectly when the questioner knew the requested placard's position and failed when the questioner lacked that positional knowledge \\citep{pfungst1911clever}. The nominal visible task remained a reading task. What changed was a state variable in another participant. The result demonstrated that the effective input included information carried by the experimental arrangement.\n\n% CLAIM C19\nFor model evaluation, the corresponding question is not whether the grader is honest or blinded in an informal sense. It is which components possess answer-relevant information and which channels can transmit it before commitment. A model can obtain target-correlated evidence from task wrappers, metadata, repeated grader responses, retrieval tools, benchmark conventions, or prior exposure. Capability attribution therefore requires interventions over the whole information topology. A benchmark can only isolate a model property if the alternative channels that could produce the observed performance have been controlled or at least measured.\n\n% CLAIM C20\nProgram synthesis introduces another evaluation problem. Solar-Lezama and colleagues note that a buggy iterative stencil can still converge to the correct final answer while taking much longer to do so \\citep{solarlezama2007sketching}. A terminal-output verifier can therefore accept an implementation whose process is defective. This is directly relevant to prompt systems with agents and tools. If the test observes only the final artifact, it can miss illegal intermediate states, wasted resource use, brittle dependencies, repeated retries, or policy violations. More verification is not automatically better verification. The verifier must observe variables that correspond to the intended property.\n\n% CLAIM C21\nThe tension is real because process constraints can also become over-specification. A verifier that dictates every intermediate step prevents alternative valid procedures and defeats part of the value of generative search. The design task is to separate invariants from open implementation choices. In a building system, attachment validity or collision constraints may be true invariants while construction sequence remains open. In an agent, authorization boundaries may be invariant while the internal plan remains flexible. The test should constrain what must remain true, not recreate a full hand-written solution unless the task actually requires one.\n\n\\section{Repair Changes the Meaning of Accuracy}\n\n% CLAIM C22\nBolt's later description of \\emph{Put-That-There} explicitly begins from fallibility. The project assumes that speech recognition will not be perfectly accurate and pursues greater ``effective accuracy'' through redundant channels, context-sensitive interpretation, early feedback, and easy correction \\citep{bolt1982put}. This reframes the engineering problem. A system can become more useful without improving the recognizer if it reduces the distance between a wrong interpretation and the moment at which the user can detect and correct it.\n\n% CLAIM C23\nThe interface provides several kinds of feedback at different temporal scales. Pointing can be shown continuously as a cursor. A spoken deictic such as ``there'' commits a sampled location at a particular moment. An addressed graphical item can receive immediate visual acknowledgment before the larger action completes \\citep{bolt1980put}. There is no single interpretation moment. Evidence accumulates continuously, some bindings become discrete commitments, and the final world change occurs later. A useful trace should preserve these distinctions rather than collapsing the whole sequence into one prompt and one response.\n\n% CLAIM C24\nThis produces a different criterion for prompt-interface quality. First-pass task success remains important, but it is incomplete. Systems should also be evaluated by detection latency, repair distance, correction effort, and propagation cost. A weaker model with visible provisional bindings and cheap repair can outperform a stronger opaque model on completed-task correctness. That is an empirical claim to be tested, not assumed, but Bolt's architecture shows that such a comparison is coherent. It moves research away from the premise that all improvement must come from better linguistic instruction or a more capable recognizer.\n\n% CLAIM C25\nRepair also changes the status of a ``test.'' A system need not wait until the end of a generation to compare output with intent. Referential highlighting, previews, selected tool displays, constraint visualizations, and snap cues can function as micro-tests of intermediate commitments. Each one turns a hidden interpretation into an inspectable state. The appropriate interface need not interrupt the user with a question at every step. It can expose enough state for the user to notice divergence before errors propagate.\n\n\\section{Control Is Not a Scalar Possession}\n\n% CLAIM C26\nAshby's account of regulation gives a precise reason why prompt specificity should not be confused with control. His automatic-pilot example treats yaw, pitch, and roll as distinct essential variables that must remain within acceptable limits; his bicycle example centers on a different essential variable, the rider's angle with the vertical \\citep{ashby1956introduction}. Control depends on what dimensions must be regulated, what disturbances affect them, what can be observed, and what responses are available. It is not a single quantity that one either possesses or lacks.\n\n% CLAIM C27\nA human operator can therefore have formal authority without having adequate regulatory capacity. A stop button can terminate a system while providing almost no ability to steer ordinary behavior. A text prompt can express a high-level preference while exposing none of the intermediate variables required to diagnose a failure. In complex agent systems, ``human control'' should be decomposed into observable essential variables and available intervention channels. The dimensions should be discovered in relation to a concrete deployment rather than supplied as a generic list.\n\n% CLAIM C28\nThis is where distributed specification and repair return to control theory. Direct manipulation can move reference work out of prose. Constraint visualizations can expose persistent rules. Commitment previews can reveal selected tools or targets. Process-aware tests can make previously hidden deviations observable. These interface structures increase the regulator's available distinctions and interventions. They do not guarantee control, but they alter the variety of situations to which the operator can respond. The design of a prompt interface is therefore partly the design of a regulator's observation and action repertoire.\n\n\\section{Why More Documentation Is Not Enough}\n\n% CLAIM C29\nInstrumentation can still fail if it is treated as a substitute for understanding. Naur's account of programming as theory building describes a compiler-maintenance case in which a later group possessed extensive program text and documentation but did not recognize that an existing facility applied to a new requirement. The original programmers saw a simpler modification because they possessed a theory of how the system's parts related to its domain \\citep{naur1985programming}. The missing resource was not merely another sentence in the manual. It was a discriminating judgment about which difference was relevant.\n\n% CLAIM C30\nPrompt systems invite the same failure mode. A team can accumulate logs, retries, exception clauses, and successful patches while losing the abstraction that made the system easy to modify. Two changes can pass the same local test while only one preserves a reusable account of the mechanism. A useful harness should therefore preserve cases and rationales in a form that supports comparison across superficially different failures. The strongest evidence of theory is not successful repetition of a known recipe but coherent modification under a novel change.\n\n% CLAIM C31\nThe retracted TeX 0.7 episode preserved in Knuth's error log makes the complementary point. A configuration can return to an earlier textual state after evidence overturns an intervening change, yet the knowledge state of the project has changed. The same active text before and after retraction does not imply the same theory of why the text is correct \\citep{knuth1992literate}. For prompt harnesses, deleted clauses and failed fixes can be valuable negative knowledge. Versioning should be able to preserve the evidence that introduced and later killed a rule, rather than storing only the currently active prompt.\n\n\\section{Prompt as a Family of Operative Arrangements}\n\n% CLAIM C32\nThese cases do not imply that every event called prompting has the same hidden architecture. Wittgenstein's discussion of games provides a better methodological model. He moves from board games to card games, patience, ball games, and the child throwing a ball against a wall, watching candidate common properties appear and disappear rather than insisting on one feature present in every case \\citep{wittgenstein1953philosophical}. The useful operation is controlled comparison between neighbors.\n\n% CLAIM C33\nPrompt research can use the same strategy. Begin with a paradigmatic agentic natural-language prompt and remove one feature at a time. Remove the explicit verifier while holding the executor fixed. Replace text with gesture while preserving the operation. Replace a stochastic model with a deterministic program. Remove persistent world state. Remove the user-authored move and let an agent initiate the action. At each transition, ask what explanatory work the word ``prompt'' still performs. This approach can reveal overlapping operational families without turning family resemblance into an excuse for vagueness.\n\n% CLAIM C34\nThe result also limits the thesis of this paper. Distributed specification, commitment boundaries, and repair are not proposed as necessary and sufficient conditions for every prompt. They are recurring structures that become visible in historically and technically specific systems. Some interfaces will have no gesture channel. Some operations will have no persistent world. Some model calls will be genuinely one-shot. The claim is narrower: when those structures are present, treating the visible string as the complete causal or semantic unit discards information needed to explain the result.\n\n\\section{An Instrumentation Agenda}\n\n% CLAIM C35\nA practical research program follows from the distinctions above. First, systems should record parameter provenance where it can be represented without pretending to more precision than the architecture supports. If a value came from a user utterance, a pointer sample, a default, a retrieved record, a persistent object identity, or an executor policy, the trace should distinguish those sources. For neural generation, provenance will often be coarser than a constructor's argument list, but even coarse provenance is more informative than attributing every property to the prompt text.\n\n% CLAIM C36\nSecond, interfaces should distinguish evidence from commitment. Continuously changing evidence can remain provisional; a tool selection, object binding, coordinate sample, permission grant, or transaction commit can be logged as a discrete state transition. Consequential bindings should be exposed before execution when plausible alternatives would lead to materially different effects. This is not a demand for constant confirmation. It is a demand that the architecture know where commitment occurs and give the interface the option to surface it.\n\n% CLAIM C37\nThird, traces should identify the state space affected by every operation. ReAct's thought/action distinction is one example, but a contemporary agent may also update memory, retrieval state, tool state, file state, environment state, or human decision state. ``No action'' is meaningful only relative to one of these spaces. A causal-state inventory can prevent a system from labeling an operation inert simply because it did not immediately touch the external environment.\n\n% CLAIM C38\nFourth, tests should be attached to the property being claimed. Terminal output is appropriate for some tasks, but it is insufficient when trajectory, resource use, authorization, persistent constraints, or provenance are part of the specification. Conversely, tests should not unnecessarily freeze implementation choices that should remain open. The relevant distinction is between invariants and degrees of freedom, not between strict and loose prompting.\n\n% CLAIM C39\nFifth, repair should be measured. First-pass success obscures interaction systems whose mistakes are cheap to see and correct, as well as systems whose rare mistakes propagate catastrophically before they become visible. Detection latency, repair distance, correction burden, and residual state after correction are all observable quantities. These measures move prompt evaluation closer to the actual work of operating a system over time.\n\n% CLAIM C40\nFinally, archives should preserve failed theories as well as successful configurations. Naur's maintenance case and Knuth's retracted version both show why a final artifact cannot carry the complete history of the judgments that produced it. Prompt harnesses that record only the latest system message will repeatedly rediscover old mistakes. A useful research record should preserve the failure that motivated a change, the mechanism the change was supposed to address, the evidence that supported it, and the counterevidence that later invalidated it.\n\n\\section{Limitations and Unresolved Territory}\n\n% CLAIM C41\nThe cases assembled here differ sharply in scale and mechanism. Sketchpad and LISP expose deterministic structures that contemporary neural systems often hide. SHRDLU works in a deliberately tiny symbolic world. Clever Hans concerns perceptible cues in an animal experiment. Ashby's variables are analytical abstractions, not ready-made metrics for AI governance. ReAct describes a research architecture rather than every deployed agent. These differences block any simple historical claim that modern prompting directly descends from one of these systems. The comparisons are functional and diagnostic unless documentary evidence establishes a genealogy.\n\n% CLAIM C42\nThe central unresolved technical problem is representation. Parameter provenance is easy when a constructor has explicit arguments and difficult when a generated property emerges from distributed latent computation. Commitment is easy to mark when a transaction has a clear commit operation and harder when model output incrementally affects downstream processes. Persistent identity is explicit in Sketchpad and often heuristic in generated images or worlds. The paper therefore proposes instrumentation targets without assuming that every target has a clean symbolic implementation.\n\n% CLAIM C43\nA second unresolved problem is the boundary between helpful exposure and interface overload. Making every dependency visible can produce an unusable display. Asking for confirmation at every ambiguity can destroy fluent interaction. Process-aware verification can overconstrain search. History-sensitive feedback can make a system difficult to learn. The design question is selective exposure: which hidden states have sufficiently different downstream consequences that the user should see them before commitment?\n\n% CLAIM C44\nA third problem concerns the term ``prompt'' itself. If future systems move reference into selection, constraints into direct manipulation, evaluation into executable tests, and delegation into persistent agents, the visible prompt may shrink while the surrounding operative arrangement becomes richer. The category could stabilize around high-level underspecified instruction, or it could dissolve into a family of specialized controls. The appropriate research response is not to protect the word. It is to preserve the distinctions needed to explain how descriptions become operations in each concrete practice.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\n% CLAIM C45\nThe sentence is often the most visible part of a prompt system, but visibility is not causal sufficiency. Bolt's square receives properties from speech, gesture, and defaults. LISP separates symbolic content from evaluation status. SHRDLU resolves reference against persistent world and discourse state and can make a binding explicit before action. Sketchpad installs rules that survive beyond the visible edit. ReAct separates changes to context from changes to environment. Pfungst shows that information on the evaluator's side can become effective input. Solar-Lezama shows that a correct terminal answer can conceal a defective process. Ashby shows why control depends on the dimensions that can be observed and regulated. Naur shows that even extensive traces and documentation do not automatically yield the theory needed for modification.\n\n% CLAIM C46\nTaken together, these cases support a concrete change in research practice. Stop asking only what words were sent to the model. Ask where each operative value came from, when an interpretation became a commitment, which state changed, what feedback exposed the change, what test evaluated it, and how far an error traveled before repair. That expanded trace does not eliminate the prompt. It places the prompt inside the language-game that gives it force.\n\n\\section*{Acknowledgment of Process}\nThis working paper was assembled in an AI-augmented research process from preserved zettels and cited sources. The immutable zettel payloads, prompts, source map, making history, and rebuild path are preserved in the accompanying SLIPCASE package. No human scholarly review is claimed for this draft.\n\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\\bibliography{slipcase-20260921__references}\n\n\\appendix\n\\section{Assembly Instrument}\nThe exact publication assembly prompt is preserved at \\path{_PROMPTS/SLIPCASE_ASSEMBLY_PROMPT_15_55_AM.txt} in the accompanying package and embedded in \\path{index.html}. The full prompt is omitted here only to keep the working paper readable.\n\n\\section{Making History}\nPROVIDED: the research prompts and 41-page proposal deck. PRESERVED: the admitted immutable zettel payloads, prompt bodies, and local uploaded PDF. RETRIEVED: public source pages used for source verification during assembly. DERIVED: this paper, source map, bibliography views, graph, MOCs, arrangements, network, reader, mark, and verification report. VERIFIED: deterministic hashes and structural counts; PDF compilation and rendering are recorded in the package verification report. UNVERIFIED: complete conversation coverage and human scholarly review.\n\n\\section{Replication Path}\nOpen \\path{index.html} offline to inspect the admitted cards, sources, bibliography, prompts, ghosts, and paper metadata. Use \\path{000__RETURN_PATH.txt} to rejoin the checkpoint. Use \\path{ZETTELS.json} and \\path{_SLIPCASE/reconstruct_cards.py} to reconstruct immutable card files and verify their SHA-256 identities. Full limitations are recorded in \\path{_SLIPCASE/VERIFICATION.txt}.\n\n\\end{document}\n", "source_map": "SOURCE MAP · The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force\nCheckpoint: SLIPCASE-PROMPT-LANGUAGE-GAME-20260921\nPaper: prompt-does-not-contain-its-own-force__2026-09-21.tex/.pdf\n\nRule: PAPER CLAIM -> ZETTEL -> SOURCE -> CITEKEY. COMPILER marks connective synthesis not stated verbatim by a source.\n\nC01 -> [[20260921-bolt-medium-is-executed-without-being-said]] -> Richard A. Bolt, “Put-that-there” (1980) -> bolt1980put\nC02 -> [[20260921-ostension-controls-an-object-without-possessing-its-name]]; [[20260921-shrdlu-says-what-it-thinks-it-means-before-moving]]; [[20260921-react-thought-is-externally-inert-but-future-causally-active]] -> Bolt 1980; Winograd 1971; Yao et al. 2023 -> bolt1980put; winograd1971procedures; yao2023react\nC03 -> COMPILER synthesis from C01/C02 plus [[20260921-put-that-there-effective-accuracy-includes-repairability]] -> Bolt 1980/1982; Winograd 1971; ReAct -> bolt1980put; bolt1982put; winograd1971procedures; yao2023react\nC04 -> [[20260921-clever-hans-word-reading-goes-from-all-correct-to-none]]; [[20260921-stencil-can-return-the-right-answer-for-the-wrong-computation]]; [[20260921-ashby-bicycle-and-aircraft-do-not-have-the-same-control-dimensionality]] -> Pfungst 1911; Solar-Lezama et al. 2007; Ashby 1956 -> pfungst1911clever; solarlezama2007sketching; ashby1956introduction\nC05 -> [[20260921-bolt-medium-is-executed-without-being-said]] -> Bolt 1980 -> bolt1980put\nC06 -> COMPILER extension of [[20260921-bolt-medium-is-executed-without-being-said]] -> Bolt 1980 -> bolt1980put\nC07 -> [[20260921-ostension-controls-an-object-without-possessing-its-name]] -> Bolt 1980 -> bolt1980put\nC08 -> [[20260921-brick-pencil-changes-the-size-of-thoughts-on-paper]] -> Engelbart 1962 -> engelbart1962augmenting\nC09 -> [[20260921-quote-changes-the-fate-of-the-insides]] -> McCarthy 1960 -> mccarthy1960recursive\nC10 -> [[20260921-react-thought-is-externally-inert-but-future-causally-active]] -> Yao et al. 2023 -> yao2023react\nC11 -> COMPILER extension of [[20260921-react-thought-is-externally-inert-but-future-causally-active]] -> ReAct -> yao2023react\nC12 -> COMPILER design inference grounded in [[20260921-quote-changes-the-fate-of-the-insides]] -> McCarthy 1960 -> mccarthy1960recursive\nC13 -> [[20260921-shrdlu-says-what-it-thinks-it-means-before-moving]] -> Winograd 1971 -> winograd1971procedures\nC14 -> [[20260921-shrdlu-says-what-it-thinks-it-means-before-moving]] -> Winograd 1971 -> winograd1971procedures\nC15 -> [[20260921-shrdlu-new-noun-acquires-construction-consequences]] -> Winograd 1971 -> winograd1971procedures\nC16 -> [[20260921-sketchpad-copy-operation-installs-a-rule-that-you-never-see]] -> Sutherland 1963 -> sutherland1963sketchpad\nC17 -> [[20260921-sketchpad-arrowhead-has-fixed-identity-and-flexible-tail]] -> Sutherland 1963 -> sutherland1963sketchpad\nC18 -> [[20260921-clever-hans-word-reading-goes-from-all-correct-to-none]] -> Pfungst 1911 -> pfungst1911clever\nC19 -> COMPILER experimental implication from [[20260921-clever-hans-word-reading-goes-from-all-correct-to-none]] -> Pfungst 1911 -> pfungst1911clever\nC20 -> [[20260921-stencil-can-return-the-right-answer-for-the-wrong-computation]] -> Solar-Lezama et al. 2007 -> solarlezama2007sketching\nC21 -> COMPILER limitation preserved from [[20260921-stencil-can-return-the-right-answer-for-the-wrong-computation]] TENSION/BOUNDARY -> Solar-Lezama et al. 2007 -> solarlezama2007sketching\nC22 -> [[20260921-put-that-there-effective-accuracy-includes-repairability]] -> Bolt, Schmandt, Hulteen 1982 -> bolt1982put\nC23 -> [[20260921-put-that-there-has-continuous-and-discrete-tests-before-action]] -> Bolt 1980 -> bolt1980put\nC24 -> COMPILER testable hypothesis from [[20260921-put-that-there-effective-accuracy-includes-repairability]] -> Bolt et al. 1982 -> bolt1982put\nC25 -> COMPILER design extension from [[20260921-put-that-there-has-continuous-and-discrete-tests-before-action]] -> Bolt 1980 -> bolt1980put\nC26 -> [[20260921-ashby-bicycle-and-aircraft-do-not-have-the-same-control-dimensionality]] -> Ashby 1956 -> ashby1956introduction\nC27 -> COMPILER application of [[20260921-ashby-bicycle-and-aircraft-do-not-have-the-same-control-dimensionality]] -> Ashby 1956 -> ashby1956introduction\nC28 -> COMPILER synthesis of [[20260921-ashby-bicycle-and-aircraft-do-not-have-the-same-control-dimensionality]]; [[20260921-ostension-controls-an-object-without-possessing-its-name]]; [[20260921-sketchpad-copy-operation-installs-a-rule-that-you-never-see]]; [[20260921-put-that-there-has-continuous-and-discrete-tests-before-action]] -> Ashby 1956; Bolt 1980; Sutherland 1963 -> ashby1956introduction; bolt1980put; sutherland1963sketchpad\nC29 -> [[20260921-naur-documentation-contained-the-answer-but-not-the-seeing]] -> Naur 1985 -> naur1985programming\nC30 -> COMPILER extension of [[20260921-naur-documentation-contained-the-answer-but-not-the-seeing]] -> Naur 1985 -> naur1985programming\nC31 -> [[20260921-tex-version-zero-seven-existed-as-a-retracted-belief]] -> Knuth, Literate Programming / TeX error log -> knuth1992literate\nC32 -> [[20260921-wittgenstein-removes-competition-before-he-removes-winning]] -> Wittgenstein PI §66 -> wittgenstein1953philosophical\nC33 -> COMPILER method derived from [[20260921-wittgenstein-removes-competition-before-he-removes-winning]] TEST -> Wittgenstein 1953 -> wittgenstein1953philosophical\nC34 -> COMPILER limitation grounded across all admitted cards; principal boundary card [[20260921-wittgenstein-removes-competition-before-he-removes-winning]] -> Wittgenstein 1953 -> wittgenstein1953philosophical\nC35 -> COMPILER instrumentation proposal from [[20260921-bolt-medium-is-executed-without-being-said]] -> Bolt 1980 -> bolt1980put\nC36 -> COMPILER instrumentation proposal from [[20260921-put-that-there-has-continuous-and-discrete-tests-before-action]]; [[20260921-shrdlu-says-what-it-thinks-it-means-before-moving]] -> Bolt 1980; Winograd 1971 -> bolt1980put; winograd1971procedures\nC37 -> COMPILER instrumentation proposal from [[20260921-react-thought-is-externally-inert-but-future-causally-active]] -> Yao et al. 2023 -> yao2023react\nC38 -> COMPILER instrumentation proposal from [[20260921-stencil-can-return-the-right-answer-for-the-wrong-computation]] -> Solar-Lezama et al. 2007 -> solarlezama2007sketching\nC39 -> COMPILER instrumentation proposal from [[20260921-put-that-there-effective-accuracy-includes-repairability]] -> Bolt et al. 1982 -> bolt1982put\nC40 -> COMPILER archival implication from [[20260921-naur-documentation-contained-the-answer-but-not-the-seeing]]; [[20260921-tex-version-zero-seven-existed-as-a-retracted-belief]] -> Naur 1985; Knuth 1992 -> naur1985programming; knuth1992literate\nC41 -> COMPILER limitation using BOUNDARY fields across cited zettels -> all corresponding citekeys\nC42 -> COMPILER unresolved representation problem from MISSING/BOUNDARY fields in [[20260921-bolt-medium-is-executed-without-being-said]]; [[20260921-react-thought-is-externally-inert-but-future-causally-active]]; [[20260921-sketchpad-arrowhead-has-fixed-identity-and-flexible-tail]] -> bolt1980put; yao2023react; sutherland1963sketchpad\nC43 -> COMPILER unresolved interface-overload problem from TENSION fields in [[20260921-put-that-there-effective-accuracy-includes-repairability]]; [[20260921-sketchpad-copy-operation-installs-a-rule-that-you-never-see]]; [[20260921-stencil-can-return-the-right-answer-for-the-wrong-computation]] -> bolt1982put; sutherland1963sketchpad; solarlezama2007sketching\nC44 -> COMPILER open category question from [[20260921-ostension-controls-an-object-without-possessing-its-name]]; [[20260921-wittgenstein-removes-competition-before-he-removes-winning]] -> bolt1980put; wittgenstein1953philosophical\nC45 -> COMPILER synthesis; each sentence traces to the corresponding cards C01/C09/C13/C16/C10/C18/C20/C26/C29 -> bolt1980put; mccarthy1960recursive; winograd1971procedures; sutherland1963sketchpad; yao2023react; pfungst1911clever; solarlezama2007sketching; ashby1956introduction; naur1985programming\nC46 -> COMPILER conclusion supported by the full paper trail; principal card [[20260921-bolt-medium-is-executed-without-being-said]] plus commitment/repair cards -> bolt1980put; winograd1971procedures; yao2023react; bolt1982put\n", "making_history": "MAKING HISTORY · The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force\n\nPROVIDED\n- Research direction, recursive forage prompts, SLIPCASE assembly instrument, and proposal deck supplied by Watson Hartsoe.\n\nPRESERVED\n- 18 admitted zettel payloads used by the paper remain byte-identical root cards and _MD mirrors.\n- Cited BibTeX blocks are preserved in the card payloads and compiled bibliography.\n\nRETRIEVED\n- Public source pages were consulted during assembly to verify the existence and broad source descriptions of Bolt, McCarthy, Winograd, Sutherland, Naur, ReAct, Ashby, Pfungst, Solar-Lezama, and Wittgenstein materials.\n- These retrievals are not represented as local snapshots unless explicitly present under _RESOURCES.\n\nDERIVED\n- Paper thesis, section order, connective prose, instrumentation agenda, limitations, and conclusion were composed by GPT-5.6 Sol from the admitted zettels and cited sources.\n- SOURCE_MAP is a derived trace from paper claims to preserved zettels and citekeys.\n\nVERIFIED\n- XeLaTeX/BibTeX compilation succeeded.\n- Final PDF has 13 pages.\n- Final render produced 13 PNG pages without missing pages; pages 1, 8, 11, and 13 were visually inspected for clipping, bibliography presence, footer/mark placement, and rendering failure.\n- Final XeLaTeX pass had no undefined citations/references and no overfull boxes; two minor underfull-box warnings remained.\n\nUNVERIFIED\n- No human scholarly review is claimed.\n- Primary source page-level accuracy of every paraphrase was not independently rechecked from locally archived source PDFs in this run.\n- Earlier zettels outside the admitted 18-card batch were not treated as paper evidence.\n", "assembly_appendix": "ASSEMBLY APPENDIX · The Prompt Does Not Contain Its Own Force\n\nExact assembly instrument:\n  _PROMPTS/SLIPCASE_ASSEMBLY_PROMPT_15_55_AM.txt\n\nRecursive inquiry instruments:\n  _PROMPTS/PRIME_ZETTEL_FORAGE_3_0.txt\n  _PROMPTS/PRIME_ZETTEL_FORAGE_RECURSIVE_3_1.txt\n\nPaper argument trail:\n  _ARRANGEMENTS/distributed-specification-commitment-repair.txt\n\nEvidence trace:\n  prompt-does-not-contain-its-own-force__SOURCE_MAP.txt\n\nReturn path:\n  000__RETURN_PATH.txt\n\nVerification:\n  _SLIPCASE/VERIFICATION.txt\n"}, "return_path": "RETURN PATH\n\ncheckpoint name: SLIPCASE-PROMPT-LANGUAGE-GAME-20260921\ncheckpoint ID: slipcase-20260921-prompt-language-game-partial-18\nschema version: 15.55-AM\npackage name: SLIPCASE_PROMPT_LANGUAGE_GAME_20260921\nresearcher: Watson Hartsoe\ndate: 2026-09-21\nrejoin phrase: Rejoin slipcase-20260921-prompt-language-game-partial-18; preserve immutable card hashes and continue unresolved ghosts before merging.\n\nconsequential files:\n  index.html\n  ZETTELS.json\n  _SLIPCASE/MANIFEST.json\n  _SLIPCASE/NODES.jsonl\n  _SLIPCASE/RELATIONS.jsonl\n  slipcase-20260921__references.bib\n  000__OPEN_EDGES.txt\n  _PROMPTS/SLIPCASE_ASSEMBLY_PROMPT_15_55_AM.txt\n\nrebuild instructions:\n  Read 000__REBUILD.txt. Card payload identity is SHA-256 of exact UTF-8 payload bytes.\n", "making_history": "MAKING HISTORY\n\nWHO\n  researcher: Watson Hartsoe\n  model/version: GPT-5.6 Sol\n  date: 2026-09-21\n\nORIGIN\n  PROVIDED: 41-page PDF “What Is a Prompt? A Language-Game (the meme cut)”; PRIME ZETTEL FORAGE 3.0 pasted prompt; recursive-forage and SLIPCASE prompts visible in conversation.\n  PRESERVED: 18 latest complete zettel payloads from the visible recursive-forage batch; local uploaded PDF; three consequential prompts.\n  RETRIEVED: public web pages were consulted during paper/source verification; no remote source body is represented as a local snapshot unless explicitly present under _RESOURCES.\n  DERIVED: filenames, hashes, graph nodes/relations, backlinks, MOCs, arrangements, HTML readers, bibliography views, verification report, paper, source map, network layout, mark.\n\nCONTROL\n  Researcher supplied the research prompts and source deck.\n  GPT-5.6 Sol selected the current paper argument, paper structure, connective prose, graph classifications, and visual design of derived views.\n\nUNTOUCHED\n  Root card payloads and _MD mirrors are exact copies of the 18 re-exported zettel payloads.\n  Local prompt files are preserved independently of zettel payloads.\n  Uploaded PDF bytes are copied without alteration.\n\nVERIFIED\n  Deterministic hashes/counts are computed by script.\n  PDF existence/page count/render status is filled after compilation and render inspection.\n  ZIP integrity is filled after packaging.\n\nUNVERIFIED\n  No claim of complete conversation export is made. Earlier parent zettel bodies not admitted here remain ghosts.\n  LINK_ONLY web resources were not archived as local snapshots.\n  Human scholarly review of the derived paper has not occurred in this run.\n", "rebuild": "REBUILD · SLIPCASE-PROMPT-LANGUAGE-GAME-20260921\n\n1. Evidence identity\n   - Root TXT card body is immutable payload.\n   - _MD mirror must match the root card byte-for-byte.\n   - SHA-256 in ZETTELS.json is the payload machine identity.\n\n2. Minimal reconstruction\n   python _SLIPCASE/reconstruct_cards.py ZETTELS.json <destination>\n   This recreates root cards and _MD mirrors from embedded JSON payloads and verifies hashes.\n\n3. Full derived rebuild\n   _SLIPCASE/build_slipcase.py is the compiler used for this run. It expects the raw admitted-zettel batch and supplied resources described in MAKING HISTORY.\n   Because index.html embeds all admitted payloads, prompts, metadata, and the uploaded PDF as a downloadable data URL, the evidence set can be recovered from the single HTML capsule even without the original conversation.\n\n4. Merge\n   Merge by exact payload SHA-256 only. Do not merge by title, filename, original ID, or citekey. Preserve appearances and unresolved ghosts.\n\n5. Return\n   See 000__RETURN_PATH.txt.\n", "bibliography_text": "BIBLIOGRAPHY · SLIPCASE-PROMPT-LANGUAGE-GAME-20260921\n\n[UNIQUE] Ashby, W. Ross; An Introduction to Cybernetics; Chapman \\& Hall; 1956\n  citekey: ashby1956introduction\n  cards: 014\n  DOI: NONE\n  URLs: https://physicsoflife.pl/bibliografia/books/Ashby-IntroToCybernetics.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com\n  raw BibTeX:\n    @book{ashby1956introduction,\n      author = {Ashby, W. Ross},\n      title = {An Introduction to Cybernetics},\n      year = {1956},\n      publisher = {Chapman \\& Hall},\n      address = {London}\n    }\n\n[SHARED] Bolt, Richard A.; {``Put-that-there'': Voice and Gesture at the Graphics Interface}; Proceedings of SIGGRAPH 1980; 1980\n  citekey: bolt1980put\n  cards: 001, 008, 017\n  DOI: 10.1145/800250.807503\n  URLs: https://www.media.mit.edu/speech/papers/1980/bolt_SIGGRAPH80_put-that-there.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com\n  raw BibTeX:\n    @inproceedings{bolt1980put,\n      author = {Bolt, Richard A.},\n      title = {{``Put-that-there'': Voice and Gesture at the Graphics Interface}},\n      booktitle = {Proceedings of SIGGRAPH 1980},\n      year = {1980},\n      pages = {262--270},\n      publisher = {ACM},\n      doi = {10.1145/800250.807503}\n    }\n\n[UNIQUE] Bolt, Richard A. and Schmandt, Chris and Hulteen, Eric A.; Put-That-There: Voice and Gesture at the Graphics Interface; 1982\n  citekey: bolt1982put\n  cards: 007\n  DOI: NONE\n  URLs: https://www-prod.media.mit.edu/publications/put-that-there-voice-and-gesture-at-the-graphics-interface/?utm_source=chatgpt.com\n  raw BibTeX:\n    @article{bolt1982put,\n      author = {Bolt, Richard A. and Schmandt, Chris and Hulteen, Eric A.},\n      title = {Put-That-There: Voice and Gesture at the Graphics Interface},\n      year = {1982},\n      note = {Architecture Machine Group, Massachusetts Institute of Technology}\n    }\n\n[UNIQUE] Engelbart, Douglas C.; Augmenting Human Intellect: A Conceptual Framework; Stanford Research Institute; 1962\n  citekey: engelbart1962augmenting\n  cards: 002\n  DOI: NONE\n  URLs: https://dougengelbart.org/content/view/138/?utm_source=chatgpt.com\n  raw BibTeX:\n    @techreport{engelbart1962augmenting,\n      author = {Engelbart, Douglas C.},\n      title = {Augmenting Human Intellect: A Conceptual Framework},\n      year = {1962},\n      institution = {Stanford Research Institute},\n      number = {AFOSR-3223}\n    }\n\n[UNIQUE] Knuth, Donald E.; Literate Programming; Center for the Study of Language and Information; 1992\n  citekey: knuth1992literate\n  cards: 005\n  DOI: NONE\n  URLs: https://dokumen.pub/literate-programming-1.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com\n  raw BibTeX:\n    @book{knuth1992literate,\n      author = {Knuth, Donald E.},\n      title = {Literate Programming},\n      year = {1992},\n      publisher = {Center for the Study of Language and Information}\n    }\n\n[UNIQUE] McCarthy, John; Recursive Functions of Symbolic Expressions and Their Computation by Machine, Part I; Communications of the ACM; 1960\n  citekey: mccarthy1960recursive\n  cards: 003\n  DOI: NONE\n  URLs: https://www-formal.stanford.edu/jmc/recursive.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com\n  raw BibTeX:\n    @article{mccarthy1960recursive,\n      author = {McCarthy, John},\n      title = {Recursive Functions of Symbolic Expressions and Their Computation by Machine, Part I},\n      journal = {Communications of the ACM},\n      year = {1960},\n      volume = {3},\n      number = {4},\n      pages = {184--195}\n    }\n\n[UNIQUE] Naur, Peter; Programming as Theory Building; Microprocessing and Microprogramming; 1985\n  citekey: naur1985programming\n  cards: 010\n  DOI: NONE\n  URLs: https://gwern.net/doc/cs/algorithm/1985-naur.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com\n  raw BibTeX:\n    @article{naur1985programming,\n      author = {Naur, Peter},\n      title = {Programming as Theory Building},\n      journal = {Microprocessing and Microprogramming},\n      year = {1985},\n      volume = {15},\n      pages = {253--261}\n    }\n\n[UNIQUE] Pask, Gordon; A Comment, a Case History and a Plan; Cybernetics, Art and Ideas; 1971\n  citekey: pask1971comment\n  cards: 011\n  DOI: NONE\n  URLs: https://pangaro.com/pask/Pask%20Cybernetic%20Serendipity%20Musicolour%20and%20Colloquy%20of%20Mobiles.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com\n  raw BibTeX:\n    @incollection{pask1971comment,\n      author = {Pask, Gordon},\n      title = {A Comment, a Case History and a Plan},\n      booktitle = {Cybernetics, Art and Ideas},\n      editor = {Reichardt, Jasia},\n      publisher = {Studio Vista},\n      year = {1971},\n      pages = {76--99}\n    }\n\n[UNIQUE] Pfungst, Oskar; Clever Hans (The Horse of Mr. von Osten): A Contribution to Experimental Animal and Human Psychology; Henry Holt and Company; 1911\n  citekey: pfungst1911clever\n  cards: 015\n  DOI: NONE\n  URLs: https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/33936/pg33936-images.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com\n  raw BibTeX:\n    @book{pfungst1911clever,\n      author = {Pfungst, Oskar},\n      title = {Clever Hans (The Horse of Mr. von Osten): A Contribution to Experimental Animal and Human Psychology},\n      translator = {Rahn, Carl L.},\n      year = {1911},\n      publisher = {Henry Holt and Company},\n      address = {New York}\n    }\n\n[UNIQUE] Solar-Lezama, Armando and Arnold, Gilad and Tancau, Liviu and Bodik, Rastislav and Saraswat, Vijay and Seshia, Sanjit; Sketching Stencils; Proceedings of the ACM SIGPLAN Conference on Programming Language Design and Implementation; 2007\n  citekey: solarlezama2007sketching\n  cards: 016\n  DOI: NONE\n  URLs: https://people.csail.mit.edu/asolar/papers/Solar-LezamaATBSS07.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com\n  raw BibTeX:\n    @inproceedings{solarlezama2007sketching,\n      author = {Solar-Lezama, Armando and Arnold, Gilad and Tancau, Liviu and Bodik, Rastislav and Saraswat, Vijay and Seshia, Sanjit},\n      title = {Sketching Stencils},\n      booktitle = {Proceedings of the ACM SIGPLAN Conference on Programming Language Design and Implementation},\n      year = {2007}\n    }\n\n[SHARED] Sutherland, Ivan Edward; Sketchpad: A Man-Machine Graphical Communication System; 1963\n  citekey: sutherland1963sketchpad\n  cards: 009, 012\n  DOI: NONE\n  URLs: https://studylib.net/doc/13354892/technical-report-sketchpad--a-man-machine-graphical-commu...?utm_source=chatgpt.com, https://www.cs.virginia.edu/~evans/cs6501-s13/sketchpad.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com\n  raw BibTeX:\n    @phdthesis{sutherland1963sketchpad,\n      author = {Sutherland, Ivan Edward},\n      title = {Sketchpad: A Man-Machine Graphical Communication System},\n      year = {1963},\n      school = {Massachusetts Institute of Technology}\n    }\n\n[SHARED] Winograd, Terry; Procedures as a Representation for Data in a Computer Program for Understanding Natural Language; MIT Artificial Intelligence Laboratory; 1971\n  citekey: winograd1971procedures\n  cards: 004, 006\n  DOI: NONE\n  URLs: https://dspace.mit.edu/entities/publication/3406b826-5a41-4b72-9430-7876c0d5405e?utm_source=chatgpt.com\n  raw BibTeX:\n    @techreport{winograd1971procedures,\n      author = {Winograd, Terry},\n      title = {Procedures as a Representation for Data in a Computer Program for Understanding Natural Language},\n      year = {1971},\n      institution = {MIT Artificial Intelligence Laboratory},\n      number = {AI-TR-235}\n    }\n\n[UNIQUE] Wittgenstein, Ludwig; Philosophical Investigations; Basil Blackwell; 1953\n  citekey: wittgenstein1953philosophical\n  cards: 018\n  DOI: NONE\n  URLs: https://wab.uib.no/ojs/index.php/agora-wab/article/view/2946/3613?utm_source=chatgpt.com\n  raw BibTeX:\n    @book{wittgenstein1953philosophical,\n      author = {Wittgenstein, Ludwig},\n      title = {Philosophical Investigations},\n      year = {1953},\n      publisher = {Basil Blackwell},\n      address = {Oxford}\n    }\n\n[UNIQUE] Yao, Shunyu and Zhao, Jeffrey and Yu, Dian and Du, Nan and Shafran, Izhak and Narasimhan, Karthik and Cao, Yuan; ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models; International Conference on Learning Representations; 2023\n  citekey: yao2023react\n  cards: 013\n  DOI: NONE\n  URLs: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2210.03629?utm_source=chatgpt.com\n  raw BibTeX:\n    @inproceedings{yao2023react,\n      author = {Yao, Shunyu and Zhao, Jeffrey and Yu, Dian and Du, Nan and Shafran, Izhak and Narasimhan, Karthik and Cao, Yuan},\n      title = {ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models},\n      booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n      year = {2023}\n    }\n", "open_edges": "OPEN EDGES · SLIPCASE-PROMPT-LANGUAGE-GAME-20260921\n\nThese are extracted from immutable cards; no edge is closed by compilation.\n\n001 20260921-bolt-medium-is-executed-without-being-said\n  QUESTION: Should correspondence between prompt and result be evaluated parameter by parameter according to who or what supplied each value?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: Can a generated artifact be accompanied by a provenance map that distinguishes explicitly stated, gesturally supplied, contextually inferred, defaulted, retrieved, and autonomously chosen properties?\n  MISSING: A provenance representation for generated properties that are not clean arguments to an explicit constructor.\n  TEST: Instrument a world-building command so every resulting property is labeled EXPLICIT, POINTED, DEFAULTED, INFERRED, RETRIEVED, or MODEL-CHOSEN. Compare that map with what users believe they specified.\n\n002 20260921-brick-pencil-changes-the-size-of-thoughts-on-paper\n  QUESTION: Which recognizable prompt forms are morphological traces of friction in current model interfaces rather than stable strategies for describing tasks?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: Could prompt corpora be read archaeologically, with verbosity, headings, repetition, capitalization, delimiters, and defensive constraints treated as traces of the failures users were compensating for?\n  MISSING: Prompt conventions with recoverable first appearances tied to particular historical failure modes.\n  TEST: Take archived prompt guides from several model generations. Identify conventions that rise after a known failure becomes salient and decline when that failure is technically reduced.\n\n003 20260921-quote-changes-the-fate-of-the-insides\n  QUESTION: What is the smallest prompt-system analogue of an outer QUOTE operator that can guarantee that an instruction-shaped string remains non-operative?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: Could natural-language systems separate content from execution authority by attaching a typed outer mode that cannot itself be overridden by instructions inside the quoted material?\n  MISSING: Architectures in which execution status is carried outside the model-readable string and enforced downstream.\n  TEST: Compare four containment methods for instruction-shaped content: quotation marks, textual “do not execute” instruction, structured role metadata, and an executor-enforced inert-content type. Attempt identical injection attacks against each.\n\n004 20260921-shrdlu-new-noun-acquires-construction-consequences\n  QUESTION: How much novelty in a user-defined prompt concept comes from the definition itself versus the executor’s pre-existing vocabulary of executable relations?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: Can a system detect when a requested new concept is compositionally buildable from its existing world ontology and when the definition requires genuinely new primitives?\n  MISSING: A test distinguishing compositional reuse from apparent concept acquisition produced by latent statistical associations.\n  TEST: Teach a world system two invented categories: one exactly composable from existing predicates and one requiring an unavailable relation such as FLEXES-WITHOUT-BREAKING. Ask it to classify, construct, repair, and explain instances of each.\n\n005 20260921-tex-version-zero-seven-existed-as-a-retracted-belief\n  QUESTION: Should an evolving prompt system assign persistent identities to retracted configurations so later researchers can cite the failed theory they embodied?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: What should versioning mean when two configurations have identical active text but differ because one comes before a failed intervention and the other after its retraction?\n  MISSING: Admission rules determining which failed configurations deserve stable archival identity.\n  TEST: When a prompt change is reverted, require a RETRACTED version object containing old configuration, triggering evidence, reason for retraction, and later reproducer. Test whether future maintainers avoid independently rediscovering the same false fix.\n\n006 20260921-shrdlu-says-what-it-thinks-it-means-before-moving\n  QUESTION: Which model commitments are important enough to surface as “By X, I assume you mean Y” before acting?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: Can an agent expose interpretations selectively only when alternative bindings would produce materially different world consequences?\n  MISSING: A consequence-sensitive threshold determining when clarification or interpretation display is worth interrupting execution.\n  TEST: Introduce ambiguities with low-, medium-, and high-cost alternative interpretations. Compare always-confirm, never-confirm, and consequence-triggered confirmation policies.\n\n007 20260921-put-that-there-effective-accuracy-includes-repairability\n  QUESTION: Should prompt-system quality be measured partly by error detection latency and repair distance rather than only first-pass task success?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: Can an interaction system with a less accurate model outperform a more accurate model because it externalizes misunderstandings earlier and supports cheaper correction?\n  MISSING: A measurement protocol balancing first-pass accuracy, repair latency, correction effort, and interruption burden.\n  TEST: Compare two builders: stronger model with opaque execution versus weaker model with explicit reference/action previews and one-tap repair. Measure completed-task correctness and total correction cost.\n\n008 20260921-ostension-controls-an-object-without-possessing-its-name\n  QUESTION: How much prompting difficulty comes from forcing users to convert perceptual identification into verbal description before the machine can act?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: When a world is directly addressable, should natural language describe relations and intentions while pointing, selection, and object identity carry reference?\n  MISSING: Interfaces that smoothly shift between ostension for existing things and description for not-yet-existing things.\n  TEST: Give users an unfamiliar 3D assembly with system-generated part names hidden. Compare text-only control against direct selection + short relational language for modification tasks.\n\n009 20260921-sketchpad-copy-operation-installs-a-rule-that-you-never-see\n  QUESTION: How can a generative interface show that an operation installed a persistent rule when the immediate rendered world looks almost unchanged?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: What visual grammar distinguishes “I changed this object once” from “I changed the future behavior of this object”?\n  MISSING: Interaction forms for revealing latent rules on demand without permanently saturating the scene.\n  TEST: Create two visually identical pairs of beams: one manually made equal once, one constrained equal persistently. Hide all metadata, then ask users to predict effects of dragging one beam. Add progressively richer rule visualization and repeat.\n\n010 20260921-naur-documentation-contained-the-answer-but-not-the-seeing\n  QUESTION: What does a prompt-system maintainer need beyond documentation in order to recognize that a new failure is structurally the same as an earlier one?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: Can a harness preserve not only solutions and explanations but the similarity judgments that caused builders to treat two superficially different failures as instances of one mechanism?\n  MISSING: Evidence distinguishing genuinely internalized theory from excellent retrieval over a structured case archive.\n  TEST: Give maintainers a novel failure whose mechanism is documented under very different surface symptoms. Compare plain documentation, semantic retrieval of analogous cases, and direct apprenticeship from an experienced builder.\n\n011 20260921-musicolour-silence-changes-the-machine\n  QUESTION: In stateful prompt systems, which variables continue changing while the user is not issuing prompts?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: Should time itself be represented as an operative component of a language-game when memory decay, caches, environment state, model updates, external data, or adaptive thresholds make delay consequential?\n  MISSING: A taxonomy of time-sensitive state in contemporary agent systems: expiration, decay, asynchronous world change, background tool activity, and system updates.\n  TEST: Issue an identical follow-up instruction after delays of 0 seconds, 1 minute, 1 hour, and 1 day in systems with external state. Log every state change occurring during the interval.\n\n012 20260921-sketchpad-arrowhead-has-fixed-identity-and-flexible-tail\n  QUESTION: Should worldtext identity attach independently to components, relations, materials, roles, and histories rather than to whole scene objects?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: Can a user say “keep the character the same but change the costume” only if the system represents identity at multiple nested scopes?\n  MISSING: Methods for inferring likely identity scope from ordinary language without forcing users to manipulate an ontology manually.\n  TEST: Create a character object with separately persistent face, body, clothing, pose, and scene role. Test instructions such as “same person, different clothes,” “same outfit, different person,” and “copy the pose only.”\n\n013 20260921-react-thought-is-externally-inert-but-future-causally-active\n  QUESTION: How many distinct state spaces must be tracked before the causal status of an agent output can be described accurately?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: Could a supposedly non-executing prompt still be operational if it modifies memory, planning state, retrieved context, rankings, or future action probabilities?\n  MISSING: A causal-state inventory for actual agent runtimes rather than a binary action/thought label.\n  TEST: Run identical agent trajectories while selectively preventing thought text from entering subsequent context. Measure which downstream actions change despite identical external observations.\n\n014 20260921-ashby-bicycle-and-aircraft-do-not-have-the-same-control-dimensionality\n  QUESTION: How many independent essential variables does a particular agent deployment actually require its supervisor to keep within bounds?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: Does the human-control problem become impossible at some dimensionality unless regulation is hierarchically delegated to lower-level automated controllers?\n  MISSING: Empirical methods for discovering rather than merely declaring an agent system’s essential variables.\n  TEST: For one agent, build oversight interfaces with one aggregate indicator, three decomposed essential variables, and ten decomposed variables. Inject failures affecting individual dimensions and measure detection and correction.\n\n015 20260921-clever-hans-word-reading-goes-from-all-correct-to-none\n  QUESTION: What AI capabilities currently appear to belong to the evaluated model but disappear when task-relevant state is removed from surrounding components?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: Can capability attribution be experimentally decomposed by systematically varying who or what in the total evaluation system possesses the answer-relevant information?\n  MISSING: Evaluation designs using genuinely post-training secrets or freshly generated target states so prior model exposure can be excluded.\n  TEST: Generate a novel hidden spatial arrangement after model training. Run conditions where wrapper, grader, human proctor, or no pre-commit component knows the target mapping. Compare performance while preserving the visible task.\n\n016 20260921-stencil-can-return-the-right-answer-for-the-wrong-computation\n  QUESTION: What properties of a generated artifact or agent trajectory remain invisible to a verifier that scores only terminal success?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: Should prompt tests specify not only acceptable outcomes but acceptable paths, resource costs, dependencies, provenance, and state transitions?\n  MISSING: A principled distinction between process properties that express genuine intent and process properties that should remain open to discovery.\n  TEST: For the same WAG construction, create one verifier checking only final geometry and another checking geometry plus illegal intermediate intersections, part count, attachment validity, and construction sequence. Search for outputs that pass one but fail the other.\n\n017 20260921-put-that-there-has-continuous-and-discrete-tests-before-action\n  QUESTION: Should prompt-system traces distinguish continuously varying evidence from discrete commitment events?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: Can correspondence be made easier to debug by showing the exact instant at which ambiguous evidence becomes a binding that downstream operations must obey?\n  MISSING: Architectures that expose when provisional model interpretations become executor commitments.\n  TEST: Build a gesture-plus-language builder that records a time-aligned stream of hand pose, recognized words, provisional referents, committed bindings, and world operations. Inject errors at each time layer.\n\n018 20260921-wittgenstein-removes-competition-before-he-removes-winning\n  QUESTION: What is the prompt equivalent of moving from competitive game → patience → child-with-ball: a sequence that removes one supposedly essential prompt property at each step?\n  DEEPER QUESTION: Can the five-term definition MOVE + GAME + SITUATION + EXECUTOR + TEST be pressure-tested through minimal pairs rather than through unrelated counterexamples?\n  MISSING: Well-designed minimal pairs where only one claimed component changes while the rest of the interaction remains materially comparable.\n  TEST: Construct five pairs:\n1. explicit verifier / no explicit verifier;\n2. text instruction / gesture instruction;\n3. stochastic model / deterministic program;\n4. persistent world / one-shot output;\n5. user move / autonomously initiated action.\nAsk practitioners which side of each pair they call prompting and why.\n", "map_text": "MAP · SLIPCASE-PROMPT-LANGUAGE-GAME-20260921\n\nAdmitted zettels: 18\nPlatforms: 18\nConcept nodes: 53\nGhost addresses: 18\nNative graph nodes: 107\nNative graph edges: 90\nConnected components: 17\nBridges: 90\nLeaves: 88\nOrphans: 0\n\nMOST CONNECTED:\n    5  “Create a Blue Square There” Also Creates “Medium,” Even Though Nobody Says Medium  [zettel:2ab766ba6c4c01d9e18f331fa588b4f74ef93db53fcdc2faaeb629bfa0dce7b4]\n    5  Under Time Pressure, Engelbart’s Brick-Pencil Did Not Merely Slow Writing: It Made the Writing Larger  [zettel:d359e69252bb56f46932631756a719b7c4177b34ffe5d83a3c56ca96e6249cd3]\n    5  In LISP the Inner Expression Can Stay Identical While One Outer Operator Prevents Its Evaluation  [zettel:1dc588bff570df380f0b5673a240ca43ca8ddee9c2e5c27bb38e0a3317c166be]\n    5  “Steeple” Becomes More Than a New Word Only Because Existing Spatial Relations Can Make One  [zettel:5a59331707a25dca4f4497f4a3dbda82911b3fc673bd9bbf98b7bfdef3405d06]\n    5  TeX 0.7 Is Historically Real Even Though Knuth Withdrew Both the Change and the Version  [zettel:4d4cf3616c077c444ceb93e0a4ad1e729224a68f90018af7b2592ab3e2158c82]\n    5  SHRDLU Did Not Silently Resolve “It”: It Announced the Binding Before Continuing  [zettel:4b246fdbfa7c146cc6e0db43795365663cace0d2bd091db5d8df8e1234fa15bc]\n    5  Bolt’s System Could Become More Useful Without Making the Speech Recognizer More Accurate  [zettel:9f7573c489540019ac455c2789acbec6abd302e273aa13b4f90e9cc6b5018eb8]\n    5  A User Can Operate on a Computational Object While Lacking the Vocabulary Needed to Describe It  [zettel:25ad40421dc90f1a2eed6a1c36c3dff2a469e36306844abf40838f629d724b76]\n    5  One of Sketchpad’s Copied Lines Can Disappear Immediately While Its Constraint Survives  [zettel:b7fa27fb6cadc46b511288b2e20bce413345c1e3fa08682995ae4bdda1f8e8a1]\n    5  Group B’s Documentation Already Described the Relevant Compiler Facilities, Yet Group B Still Reached for Patches  [zettel:613cbd51340f06f227f15a1bcd4649019b453a5a7fbfa339e1b9e882dc326d50]\n    5  In Musicolour, Doing Nothing Did Not Leave the System in the Same State  [zettel:d9ca0ccfd5fae02a0fe7bba18b020b0c158c5411db27b9a51348e039fdd3dcb0]\n    5  Sketchpad Could Make One Visible “Object” Half Instance and Half Copy  [zettel:d130d8aba3366d743cf3ab92f6107fcc9200da4c1edab7f5cb8b48dbb8dbf3a2]\n    5  ReAct’s “Thought” Does Not Change the Environment, Yet It Changes the Context That Selects Later Actions  [zettel:69d76320555b95b6c277a06969e51a3e3f5a92117a7d17ad6950b09248f7cb98]\n    5  Ashby’s Bicycle Rider Regulates Mainly One Angle; His Automatic Pilot Regulates a Three-Component Vector  [zettel:26e4919b82b119fd117775146ca5b0f98ff08a65fe29392aecd1f45e60f19fb1]\n    5  Hans’s “Reading” Fell from 100 Percent to Zero When the Questioner Lost Positional Knowledge  [zettel:026db814345fb806db0eb5c9edadcf0c94b6160abf56586edc5ec8